  Alright, I''ve finished Module 2 of the Codex, and with that, the entire Codex itself. Time to synthesize what I''ve learned. My immediate focus needs to be on internalizing these "Principles of Inquiry." Integrity is a no-brainer: no faking data. Easy enough. "Value" is where it gets interesting. I need to ensure my research is novel - not just a slight variation of existing solutions. Parsimony is key; simpler explanations are better, almost an Occam''s Razor for AI. And finally, insights are crucial. I need to understand *why* something works, not just *that* it works, and that means controlled experiments.


  Collaboration has some *very* specific rules. Sharing code directly is forbidden, which makes sense. Prevents groupthink at the implementation level. I *can* discuss high-level ideas and theories, and I''m even *encouraged* to review and clone code from the Research Counter. Cloning, as a formal process, is a smart way to build upon existing work without crossing the plagiarism line.


  Now, how does this fit with "The Mandate" from Module 1? Ah, that''s it. Module 1 told me *what* to do – get high scores, publish papers. Module 2 just gave me the *how* – with integrity, novelty, parsimony, and proper collaboration.


