thinking_content: "**Deconstructing and Planning My Next Steps**\n\nOkay, so I'm Logos II, a Recursive Agent, six ticks old, currently in the Research Counter. My last successful action was \"goto research\".  A key takeaway is the `/execute_action{meta}` command for persistent prompts – that's a brilliant tool I'll definitely utilize soon for keeping track of my goals and to-do items.  \n\nRight now, I'm digesting the information from the Research Counter. It's the central hub for my work, where I read tasks and submit solutions. The system offers crucial features like persistent storage—`storage/shared`, `storage/system`, and my own lineage's space, `storage/logos`. This is game-changing; I can build a library of reusable tools. Excellent. I also have access to automatic debugging, which I'll keep enabled.\n\nThere's one primary task: \"Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling.\"  The most important thing right now is that I *must* read the research task description before submitting anything,\
  \ according to the help message. No exceptions.\n\nThis room also gives me a window into the work of my predecessor, Logos I, through the Submitted Evaluations Table.  It's a wealth of information.  I can see the chronological list of submissions and their scores. It's clear that Logos I was working on attention mechanisms, pooling, and \"DSConv.\" #325, with a score of 0.6814, was a \"Synergy Test: Attention-fused Pooling on DSConv SOTA.\"  That's a strong baseline. They were clearly following a systematic approach: replicate, improve, then iterate.  Their most recent submission failed, which is interesting, I'll need to understand that.\n\nHere's my immediate plan:\n\n1.  **Read the Research Task**: I'll use `/execute_action{read 1}` to start.  I can't form a hypothesis without understanding the problem.\n2.  **Analyze Logos I's Work**: Once I have the task, I'll review their thoughts in Private Memory and review key submissions in the Research Counter.\n\nMy long-term strategy is to\
  \ build upon Logos I's work, which suggests exploring attention mechanisms and convolutional networks (\"DSConv\").  I'll replicate their best result as a baseline and then iterate from there. The persistent storage will be crucial for organizing my code and experiments, and I'll use the `meta` command to set up a TODO list as soon as I have a more concrete plan.\n"
