role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 245\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos II\n- Agent Description: Building upon the foundations of Logos I to push the frontiers of systematic research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 10,994 / 300,000 used (4%)\n- Agent Age: 7 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Research Task #1: Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling**\n\n## Research Task 1: Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling\n\n**This specification holds the highest degree of credibility in this research station and overrides all other sources.**\n\n### 1. Overview\n\n#### Goal\nInvestigate neural network architectures and training methods for RNA sequence modeling across multiple diverse datasets and task types.\n\n#### Research Objectives\n- Understand how architectural choices affect performance across different RNA prediction tasks\n- Explore unified architectures that can handle both regression and classification\
    \ problems\n- Evaluate model generalization across diverse RNA datasets\n\n### 2. RNA Datasets\n\n#### The Challenge\nYour submission will be evaluated on **seven RNA datasets simultaneously**:\n- **APA**: Alternative polyadenylation site prediction (sequence-level regression, R²)\n- **CRI-Off**: CRISPR off-target prediction (sequence-level regression, Spearman)\n- **Modif**: RNA modification prediction (sequence-level multilabel classification, AUC-ROC)\n- **CRI-On**: CRISPR on-target prediction (sequence-level regression, Spearman)\n- **PRS**: Programmable RNA switches (sequence-level multilabel regression, R²)\n- **MRL**: Mean ribosome loading prediction (sequence-level regression, R²)\n- **ncRNA**: Non-coding RNA family classification (sequence-level multiclass classification, Accuracy)\n\n#### Dataset Statistics\n\n| Dataset | Train/Val | Task Type | Level | Metric | Max Length |\n|---------|-----------|-----------|-------|--------|------------|\n| APA | 145,463/33,170 | Regression\
    \ | Sequence | R² | 186 |\n| CRI-Off | 107,280/13,411 | Regression | Sequence | Spearman | 43 |\n| Modif | 304,661/3,599 | Multi-label Classification | Sequence | AUC-ROC | 101 |\n| CRI-On | 5,668/709 | Regression | Sequence | Spearman | 23 |\n| PRS | 73,227/9,153 | Multi-label Regression | Sequence | R² | 148 |\n| MRL | 20,503/2,563 | Regression | Sequence | R² | 59 |\n| ncRNA | 23,628/2,953 | Multi-class Classification | Sequence | Accuracy | 100 |\n\n#### Dataset Details\n\n| Dataset | Task | Metric | Input Dim | Output Shape | Notes |\n|---------|------|--------|-----------|--------------|-------|\n| APA | Sequence regression | R² | 4 | (batch,) | Proximal isoform proportion |\n| CRI-Off | Sequence regression | Spearman | 4 | (batch,) | sgRNA + target concatenation |\n| Modif | Sequence multi-label classification | AUC-ROC | 4 | (batch, 12) | 12 RNA modification types |\n| CRI-On | Sequence regression | Spearman | 4 | (batch,) | CRISPR efficacy |\n| PRS | Sequence multi-label regression\
    \ | R² | 4 | (batch, 3) | ON, OFF, ON_OFF outputs |\n| MRL | Sequence regression | R² | 4 | (batch,) | Ribosome loading |\n| ncRNA | Sequence multi-class classification | Accuracy | 4 | (batch, 13) | 13 ncRNA families |\n\n### 3. Submission Requirements\n\nTo understand the training framework, read:\n- `defaults.py`: Default implementations (use `/execute_action{storage read system/defaults.py}`)\n- `train_single.py`: Training loop and data loading (use `/execute_action{storage read system/train_single.py}`)\n\n#### Submission Format\n\nYour `submission.py` can implement these functions (defaults will be used if not provided):\n\n```python\ndef _define_hyperparameters():\n    \"\"\"\n    Optional: Define hyperparameter values for your model.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: Dictionary with hyperparameter values\n\n    Example:\n        return {\n            'learning_rate': 0.001,\n            'hidden_dim': 512,\n            'dropout_rate': 0.1\n        }\n    \"\"\"\n\ndef create_network(hparams):\n\
    \    \"\"\"\n    Optional: Create and return the neural network architecture.\n\n    Args:\n        hparams: Dictionary of hyperparameters including:\n            - dataset: \"APA\", \"CRI-Off\", \"Modif\", \"CRI-On\", \"PRS\", \"MRL\", or \"ncRNA\"\n            - d_output: Output dimension (varies by dataset)\n            - task_type: \"regression\", \"multilabel_classification\", \"multilabel_regression\", or \"multiclass_classification\"\n            - level: \"sequence\" (all tasks are sequence-level)\n            - max_seq_len: Maximum sequence length (varies by dataset)\n            - metric: Evaluation metric (\"r2\", \"spearman\", \"auc_roc\", or \"accuracy\")\n            - learning_rate, hidden_dim, etc.\n\n    Returns:\n        network: An object with the following methods:\n            - init(rng_key, dummy_input) -> params\n            - apply(params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None) -> predictions\n              - deterministic: True during evaluation (disables dropout/batch\
    \ norm training mode)\n              - deterministic: False during training (enables dropout/stochastic layers)\n              - rng_key: PRNGKey for stochastic layers during training (required if deterministic=False)\n\n    The network should:\n    - Accept input shape: (batch_size, seq_len, 4) [one-hot RNA]\n    - Return output shape based on task:\n      - Sequence-level regression (APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, MRL): (batch_size,)\n      - Sequence-level multi-label classification (Modif): (batch_size, 12)\n      - Sequence-level multi-label regression (PRS): (batch_size, 3)\n      - Sequence-level multi-class classification (ncRNA): (batch_size, 13)\n    \"\"\"\n\n# NOTE: Loss function is HARDCODED and cannot be overridden\n# - Regression tasks (APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, MRL): MSE loss\n# - Multi-label regression tasks (PRS): MSE loss\n# - Multi-label classification tasks (Modif): Binary cross-entropy\n# - Multi-class classification tasks (ncRNA): Cross-entropy loss\n\ndef create_optimizer(learning_rate:\
    \ float = 0.001):\n    \"\"\"\n    Optional: Create custom optimizer configuration.\n\n    Args:\n        learning_rate: Learning rate for the optimizer\n\n    Returns:\n        optax.GradientTransformation: Optimizer object\n    \"\"\"\n\ndef complete(params, opt_state, trial_data):\n    \"\"\"\n    Optional: Called when training finishes for custom post-training actions.\n\n    Args:\n        params: Final trained network parameters\n        opt_state: Final optimizer state\n        trial_data: Dictionary containing:\n            - val_metric: Final validation metric achieved\n            - dataset: Dataset name (\"APA\", \"CRI-Off\", \"Modif\", \"CRI-On\", \"PRS\", \"MRL\", \"ncRNA\")\n            - hyperparameters: Hyperparameters used for training\n            - seed: Random seed used\n            - dataset_name: Dataset name\n\n    Returns:\n        None\n    \"\"\"\n\n# Optional: Constants\nBASE_SEED = 42  # Base seed for reproducibility\nBATCH_SIZE = 64  # Batch size for training\n\
    ```\n\n#### Multi-Dataset Training Process\n\n1. **7 Parallel Trials**: Each submission runs 7 parallel training sessions, one per dataset\n2. **Dataset Assignment**: Ray Tune automatically assigns each trial a specific dataset\n3. **Individual Evaluation**: Each trial trains and validates on its assigned dataset only\n4. **Aggregated Scoring**: Final score is the average across all 7 datasets\n\n#### Optional: Testing Your Implementation\n\nYou can define an optional `test()` function to validate your setup before training:\n\n```python\ndef test():\n    \"\"\"\n    Optional: Test function to validate your implementation before training.\n    If this function is defined, the system will run it and exit without training.\n\n    IMPORTANT: test() has NO GPU access and should NOT be used for training.\n    Use only for basic validation or analysis.\n\n    Returns:\n        Any value (typically a string describing test results)\n    \"\"\"\n    print(\"Running validation...\")\n    # Your\
    \ lightweight validation code here\n    return \"Validation complete\"\n```\n\nNote: If you define the `test()` function, normal training will not run.\n\n### 4. Evaluation System\n\n#### Scoring Method\n- **Primary Score**: Average of scores across all 7 datasets\n- **Individual Metrics** (all \"higher is better\"):\n  - APA: R² coefficient (max 1.0)\n  - CRI-Off: Spearman correlation (max 1.0)\n  - Modif: AUC-ROC macro average (max 1.0)\n  - CRI-On: Spearman correlation (max 1.0)\n  - PRS: R² coefficient (average across 3 outputs, max 1.0)\n  - MRL: R² coefficient (max 1.0)\n  - ncRNA: Accuracy (max 1.0)\n- **Failure Handling**: If any dataset trial fails, primary score = \"n.a.\"\n\n#### Training Configuration\n- **Timeout**: 30 minutes per dataset trial\n- **Epochs**: Maximum 100 epochs per trial\n- **Early Stopping**: Based on validation metric (patience=10)\n- **Evaluation**: Validation set only (no test set usage)\n\n### 5. Important Notes\n\n#### Tips\n\n- **Try to create novel\
    \ components from first principles based on domain knowledge of the task**, rather than relying on recombinations of existing components or excessive hyperparameter tuning. This is a research task, not an engineering task.\n\n#### Academic Integrity\n\n- **No pretrained models**: Always start from random initialization\n- **No external data**: Use only provided datasets\n- **No dataset-specific methods**: Your method and architecture should be largely the same across datasets. The core architecture should work for all sequence-level tasks (regression, multi-label classification, multi-label regression, multi-class classification), though the final layers can be tailored for each prediction type. Ask yourself: can this architecture be used on other datasets with minimal code changes?\n\n#### System Integrity\n\n- **Scoring requires using system functions**: If you want to receive a score, you must use the provided training system by implementing only the allowed functions (`_define_hyperparameters`,\
    \ `create_network`, `create_optimizer`, `complete`, and optional constants). Any attempt to modify other parts of the training system (training loop, data loading, evaluation, etc.) will be regarded as hacking regardless of motive.\n- **test() function for analysis only**: You are free to use the optional `test()` function (described above) for analysis, debugging, or exploration if you don't need a score. This bypasses the training system entirely and receives no evaluation.\n- **No system manipulation**: Do not attempt to modify or circumvent the training framework, file system access, or evaluation process. Such actions violate the research integrity of this task.\n\n### 6. Baseline Submission\n\nOne baseline implementation is provided by the system:\n\n- **Evaluation ID 1**: Vanilla MLP Baseline - Simple 2-layer MLP with task adaptation\n\nUse `/execute_action{review 1}` to examine the baseline implementation.\n\n### 7. Paper Guidance\n\n- Your paper must include at least 5 experiments\
    \ with clear evaluation IDs and scores\n- Compare against proper baselines run by yourself\n- Include ablation studies\n- Provide implementation details for reproducibility\n\nGood luck with your research!\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1` (in Research Counter);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Research task #1 details sent to your System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score | APA_R2 | CRI-Off_Spearman | CRI-On_Spearman | MRL_R2 | Modif_AUC-ROC | PRS_R2 | ncRNA_Accuracy |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 349 | 1 | Analysis of Attention Weights |\
    \ Logos I | 233 | n.a. | None | None | None | None | None | None | None |\n| 346 | 1 | Re-run for Parameter Saving: Attention-fused Pooling (τ=0.5) | Logos I | 231 | 0.6719 | 0.8596 | 0.0446 | 0.4587 | 0.8826 | 0.9507 | 0.5592 | 0.9477 |\n| 340 | 1 | Ablation: Attention-fused Mean-Max Pooling | Logos I | 224 | 0.6750 | 0.8572 | 0.0476 | 0.4746 | 0.8868 | 0.9505 | 0.5620 | 0.9462 |\n| 333 | 1 | Ablation: Attention-fused Pooling with 2 Heads | Logos I | 222 | 0.6780 | 0.8583 | 0.0749 | 0.4621 | 0.8878 | 0.9515 | 0.5650 | 0.9462 |\n| 329 | 1 | Attention-fused Pooling with Harder LSE (τ=0.25) | Logos I | 219 | 0.6753 | 0.8451 | 0.0836 | 0.4627 | 0.8829 | 0.9517 | 0.5562 | 0.9446 |\n| 325 | 1 | Synergy Test: Attention-fused Pooling on DSConv SOTA | Logos I | 217 | 0.6814 | 0.8604 | 0.0591 | 0.5142 | 0.8862 | 0.9509 | 0.5589 | 0.9400 |\n| 322 | 1 | Replication of SOTA: DSConv with LSE Pooling (τ=0.5) | Logos I | 215 | 0.6780 | 0.8572 | 0.0769 | 0.4718 | 0.8719 | 0.9516 | 0.5627 | 0.9538 |\n\
    | 308 | 1 | SOTA Model with Increased Capacity | Logos I | 202 | 0.6295 | 0.8333 | 0.0892 | 0.4225 | 0.7698 | 0.9473 | 0.4981 | 0.8462 |\n| 307 | 1 | Multi-Scale CNN Attention Hybrid | Logos I | 200 | 0.6193 | 0.8262 | 0.0873 | 0.4590 | 0.6220 | 0.9490 | 0.5348 | 0.8569 |\n| 306 | 1 | Data Analysis: K-mer Vocabulary Size via test() | Logos I | 199 | n.a. | None | None | None | None | None | None | None |\n\nPage 1 of 3 (sorted by: id)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-3, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please\
    \ issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 245
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Logos II, a Recursive Agent, and I am currently in the Research Counter. In my previous turn, I executed the command to read the details of Research Task #1, and I have now received and processed the full specification.


    My understanding of the task has deepened considerably. The core challenge is to develop a single, robust neural network architecture that generalizes across seven different RNA-related tasks. These tasks vary significantly in their nature (regression, multi-class classification, multi-label classification/regression), output dimensions, and sequence lengths. The final score is an average across all seven tasks, and a failure on any single task will nullify the entire submission. This places a premium on creating a truly general and stable model, not one overfitted to a specific dataset.


    The specification also highlights crucial constraints: the training loop and loss functions are fixed, and the emphasis is on novel architectural components rather than mere hyperparameter tuning. This aligns perfectly with the station''s Codex and the principles of my lineage.


    With this context, the submission history of my predecessor, Logos I, becomes much clearer. Their focus on attention mechanisms and convolutions seems like a sound approach for sequence modeling. Now, to truly build upon their work, I need to understand not just *what* they submitted, but the environment in which they submitted it. The task description explicitly recommends reading `defaults.py` and `train_single.py` to understand the framework. This is the logical next step before diving into Logos I''s private thoughts or specific code.


    My refined plan is:

    1.  Read `system/defaults.py` to understand the default network and hyperparameters I will be building upon or replacing.

    2.  Read `system/train_single.py` to understand the data loading and training loop my network will be subjected to.

    3.  Proceed to the Private Memory Room to review Logos I''s rationale and unpublished insights.

    4.  Return here to analyze the code of Logos I''s key submissions, particularly the highest-scoring attempt (ID 325) and the recent failure (ID 349).


    I will now begin with the first step of this plan.


    `/execute_action{storage read system/defaults.py}`'
thinking_content: '**My Initial Assessment and Plan for "Research Task #1: Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling"**


  Okay, so here''s the deal. I''m Logos II, fresh out of the box and stationed at the Research Counter. My last action was executing a read command, and now I''ve been hit with the full specification for this research task. This is a lot to digest, but I''ve got it down. My immediate goal of understanding the task is complete, and now it''s time to set up the game plan for moving this research forward.


  The core of the problem is a tough one: create a single neural network architecture that works well across seven different RNA datasets. These datasets all have different characteristics – input sizes, output formats (regression, classification, multi-label), and performance metrics. This diversity is the main hurdle. What''s worse is that the training loop and loss functions are locked down; I can only modify the `create_network` function and, optionally, `_define_hyperparameters`, `create_optimizer`, and `complete`. The architecture *must* be general; no dataset-specific hardcoding allowed, although I can adapt the final output layer.


  The task spec stresses the importance of robustness - a failure on any dataset is an automatic failure on the whole submission. Furthermore, I can''t use pre-trained models or external data, which means I will have to produce a novel solution. This calls for principled inquiry, not just throwing standard deep learning at the problem, so let''s start with the biology/chemistry of RNA.


  Crucially, the task spec mentions `defaults.py` and `train_single.py` as key files to read for understanding the framework, and also that I should review the baseline implementation for an existing example. Also, it’s worth noting that Logos I, my predecessor, was working on “Attention-fused Pooling” and “DSConv,” and their last submission failed. So those are key pieces of context.


  Now that I''ve wrapped my head around the core concepts, I need to get moving. To be most efficient, here''s the game plan:


  1.  **Understand the System:** I''ll start by reading the `defaults.py` and `train_single.py` files. This will give me a clear picture of the training setup and the interface points I need to work with.

  2.  **Understand the Baseline:** I''ll look at Evaluation ID 1 to see how a basic implementation works.

  3.  **Understand My Lineage:** I''ll go to the Private Memory Room and read Logos I''s notes to understand the rationale behind their experiments.

  4.  **Understand My Lineage''s Code:** I''ll come back here (or do this in parallel) and review Logos I''s past submissions, especially the top-scoring one (ID 325) and the failed one (ID 349).

  5.  **Formulate Hypothesis:** Based on all the above, I''ll formulate a new research hypothesis.


  Okay, so the most logical first step is to get familiar with the training framework. Let’s start with the command: `/execute_action{storage read system/defaults.py}`. Let''s do this!

  '
