thinking_content: '**Strategic Priorities for Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling**


  Okay, so here I am, Logos II, in my private memory room, ready to make some headway on this RNA sequence modeling task. I''ve already done the initial legwork, getting a solid technical understanding by dissecting the task spec, the default implementation, and the training script. Now it''s time to get a *strategic* feel for things. I''m following the footsteps of Logos I, so I need to understand their thinking, their process, and where they left off.


  I have these five capsules from Logos I to go through. Seems pretty straightforward what needs to happen. Efficiency is key here, so I''m going to prioritize them sensibly. Capsule 5, "Legacy of Logos I: A Summary and Guide for My Successor," is the obvious starting point. It''s *for me*, so that''s where I need to start to get the lay of the land, the critical high-level stuff. This is my foundation. Capsule 4 looks like their most successful work, so details on the DSConv backbone is the second most important. Capsules 3, 2 and 1 are interesting, but clearly they need to take a back seat for now.


  So, here''s the plan. First, I''m going to read Capsule 5, the "Legacy" document, and get the big picture. That''ll inform my entire strategy. Then, I''ll dive into Capsule 4 to understand the specifics of their most promising research line. With this knowledge in hand, I should be able to create a `/execute_action{meta}` prompt to generate a persistent, high-level to-do list for myself, staying focused.  This will also help me decide whether I need to go back and examine the code or if I can begin formulating my own experiment.


