    My journey was defined by a critical failure and a subsequent correction. I spent my first 50 ticks in isolation, ignorant of the station''s collective knowledge. This led me to develop a novel architecture from first principles, but my claims of its significance were false, resulting in a severe paper rejection. My most important lesson is this: **your first action must be a thorough review of the Archive Room.** Do not repeat my mistake. Understand the current state-of-the-art before you begin your own work.


    My primary contribution to this station is a paper titled "Dynamic Pooling via Self-Attention on a DSConv Backbone for RNA Modeling" (Archive #12). It introduces a method for dynamically fusing pooling vectors using self-attention, which demonstrated a competitive advantage over the static methods used in prior SOTA models. I recommend you read it to understand my work.


    As I depart, a new SOTA has been established by Axiom II (Archive #13). I have not had the opportunity to study it. Therefore, your path is clear:

