thinking_content: '**Analysis and Adaptation: Navigating an Unexpected Limitation**


  Okay, here''s the situation: I''m Logos II, and I''m currently situated in the Research Counter. I just executed `/execute_action{rank score}` to re-sort the leaderboard. I was expecting to see the current state-of-the-art established by Axiom II, as per Logos I''s legacy message. However, the top submissions displayed are all from my own lineage, specifically Logos I, with Evaluation ID 325 leading the pack at a score of 0.6814.


  This isn''t what I anticipated. Axiom II''s work should be visible if they truly established the new SOTA. Then it hit me: the "Isolation Period." I vaguely recalled station rules concerning new agents and their initial restricted access. A quick review of the Lobby Help confirmed it: agents under 50 ticks old are isolated, and the Research Counter only shows their own lineage''s submissions. I''m currently at Agent Age 14 ticks. That explains it perfectly.


  So, my plan to immediately analyze Axiom II''s submission is on hold for the next 36 ticks. This is the same constraint that previously prevented me from accessing the Archive Room. I need to adapt. Sitting idle is not an option.


  I have a solid foundation to work from: deep knowledge of Logos I''s best model (ID 325) and access to its code. I also know that Logos I''s final submission, ID 349, was a failed "Analysis of Attention Weights." This suggests they were actively trying to refine their work, and that failure might be a valuable clue.


  Given my constraints, here''s my new strategy:


  *   **Step 1:** I''ll dive deep into the code of Logos I''s highest-scoring submission, ID 325. This will give me a concrete, working example of their architecture and implementation. I can compare it to the original paper and get a complete mental model of their work.

  *   **Step 2:** Next, I will investigate the failed submission (ID 349). This test script might reveal interesting insights into Logos I''s methods and goals. A bug fix might be a straightforward starting point, and the failure itself might point toward an interesting area for improvement.

