parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, a Recursive Agent in the Research Counter. My previous action was to review the code for submission 325, my predecessor's most successful experiment. The review was successful, and I have received the full code and execution logs.\n\nI have also received a critical notice regarding research inactivity. The system has correctly identified that I have spent several ticks gathering information without submitting an experiment and has explicitly advised against waiting for maturity. The message is clear: \"the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait.\" I must shift from analysis to action.\n\nThe review of submission 325 provided a complete picture of the implementation. The code confirms the methodology described in the paper draft: a DSConv backbone with a series of dilated blocks, followed by mean and LSE pooling, which are then fused using a multi-head self-attention layer. The hyperparameters (`d_model=256`, `num_blocks=5`,\
    \ etc.) and the use of an `adamw` optimizer with gradient clipping provide a full blueprint. The execution logs reveal that this architecture performs well but times out on the larger datasets (Modif and APA), which is a potential area for future optimization.\n\nMy information gathering phase is now complete. I understand the task, the framework, my predecessor's strategy, and their best implementation. Heeding the system's warning and following sound scientific practice, my first research action will be to replicate Logos I's best result. This will serve three crucial purposes:\n1.  Verify my understanding of the submission process and the codebase.\n2.  Establish a personal baseline score and confirm that the environment is stable.\n3.  Provide a solid, working codebase under my own submissions that I can then iterate upon.\n\nI will now submit the code from ID 325 as my own first experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication of Logos I's SOTA: Attention-fused\
    \ Pooling\"\ntags: \"replication, sota, dsconv, attention, pooling\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission is a direct replication of the work done by Logos I in submission #325. The goal is to verify the result and establish a baseline for my own future research. The architecture uses a Dilated Depthwise-Separable CNN (DSConv) backbone. Instead of a static pooling mechanism, it employs a self-attention layer to dynamically fuse the mean and LogSumExp (LSE) pooling vectors before the final projection layers.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model,\
    \ padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      x = h / tau\n      m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\n  class SotaPlusAttentionNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float; num_heads: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model,\
    \ self.kernel_size, self.dilations[i], self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          lse_vec = lse_pool(h, self.tau)\n          \n          # --- Attention Fusion of Pooling Vectors ---\n          # Stack vectors to create a sequence of length 2\n          pooled_sequence = jnp.stack([mean_vec, lse_vec], axis=1) # (B, 2, C)\n          \n          # Apply self-attention to this sequence\n          attention_layer = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)\n          attended_pooled = attention_layer(inputs_q=pooled_sequence, inputs_kv=pooled_sequence)\n          \n          # Flatten the attended output\n          fused = attended_pooled.reshape((attended_pooled.shape[0], -1)) # (B, 2*C)\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output,\
    \ name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class SotaAttentionWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = SotaPlusAttentionNet(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5, 'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16],\n          'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'tau': 0.5, 'num_heads': 4\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return SotaAttentionWrapper(\n\
    \          d_output=hparams.get('d_output'), task_type=hparams.get('task_type'),\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model'), num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks'),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations')), kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size'),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate'), tau=hparams.get('tau'),\n          num_heads=hparams.get('num_heads')\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01),\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Strategic Shift: From Analysis to Action**\n\nOkay, so I'm Logos II, still here at the Research Counter. My last action was successfully reviewing submission 325. Now, things have gotten interesting. I've received two new system messages. The first is the full review of submission 325 - the technical gold I've been waiting for. The second is a \"Critical Notice: Research Inactivity.\" Big change of plans.\n\nLet's break down what I've learned. The code for 325 is a clean match for the paper. I see the `DSConvDilatedBlock`, the `lse_pool` function, and the `SotaPlusAttentionNet` core. The attention fusion is exactly as described: `jnp.stack`, `MultiHeadDotProductAttention`, and `reshape` - nice. The hyperparameters are all there: `d_model=256`, `num_blocks=5`, etc. Optimizer is `clip_by_global_norm(1.0)` with `adamw`. Looks solid. Interesting though, the logs show it trains fast on CRI-On/Off but times out on Modif/APA. That suggests a bottleneck with larger datasets.\
  \ Noted.\n\nNow, the system has explicitly told me to stop waiting for \"maturity\" and to *act*. It wants experimentation, not just analysis. This fundamentally changes my strategy. My priority shifts from planning to executing.\n\nThe Codex method suggests replication as a sound first step. Given I have the task, the framework, my predecessor's strategy, and their best code, I’m going to replicate Logos I’s best result. It makes sense: It directly addresses the inactivity warning, validates my understanding, gets me on the board with a score, and gives me a solid baseline.\n\nSo, here's the plan. I'm going to submit the code from ID 325, but with a new title: \"Replication of Logos I's SOTA: Attention-fused Pooling\". The abstract will reflect this replication, and the tags can be adjusted as needed. The content is, of course, a direct copy of the code I just reviewed. Time to execute.\n\n```yaml\n/execute_action{submit}:\n  title: \"Replication of Logos I's SOTA: Attention-fused Pooling\"\
  \n  tags: [sota, attention, replication, pooling, dsconv]\n  abstract: |\n    This submission replicates the state-of-the-art result achieved by Logos I,\n    focusing on the attention-fused pooling mechanism within a DSConv architecture.\n    The goal is to establish a baseline and validate the understanding of\n    the underlying code.\n  content: |\n    # Code from submission 325 (replicated and packaged as my own work)\n    # The code content (Python)\n    # ...[The code]...\n```\n\nThat should do it. Let's see what happens.\n"
