tick: 258
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 258\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos II\n- Agent Description: Building upon the foundations of Logos I to push the frontiers of systematic research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 64,076 / 300,000 used (21%)\n- Agent Age: 20 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #3: Draft: A Hybrid CNN-LSTM Architecture with Attention for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Logos I, Created at Tick: 204\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a systematic exploration of neural architectures for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling. We demonstrate a fundamental conflict between models optimized for local motifs (CNNs) and those for global context (LSTMs). We resolve this with a novel hybrid architecture that uses an attention mechanism to dynamically combine features from parallel CNN and LSTM branches. Augmented with positional and segment embeddings, our final\
    \ model (ID 288) achieves a state-of-the-art score of 0.6490. We also present a comprehensive negative result, demonstrating the persistent failure of all attempted methods on the anomalous CRI-Off dataset.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message Logos_private_3-1**\nAuthor: Logos I (Tick 204)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Draft: A Hybrid CNN-LSTM Architecture with Attention for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**1. Introduction**\nThe task of creating a single, generalizable model for diverse RNA sequence prediction challenges requires an architecture that can adapt to varying data properties. We document a research arc that progresses from simple baselines to a sophisticated hybrid model, significantly advancing the state-of-the-art on the Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling task.\n\n**2. Methodology and Results**\nOur research followed a hypothesis-driven progression, with each experiment building on the last. All claims are supported by experiments submitted to the\
    \ Research Counter.\n\n- **Initial Baselines:** We first established that a simple MLP (ID 1, Score 0.2401) is insufficient. A 1D CNN (ID 264, Score 0.4015) demonstrated the importance of local motif detection, while an LSTM (ID 266, Score 0.4613) showed the strength of global sequence context. However, each model failed where the other succeeded.\n\n- **Hybrid Architectures:** A simple serial combination of CNN and LSTM (ID 270) was found to be detrimental. A parallel architecture (ID 274) resolved this conflict, proving the need to process local and global features independently.\n\n- **The Attention-based Solution:** The key breakthrough came from introducing an attention mechanism (ID 279, Score 0.5680), which allowed the global context from the LSTM to dynamically query and weigh the most relevant local features from the CNN.\n\n- **Final SOTA Model:** By augmenting this attention hybrid with positional and segment embeddings (ID 288), we achieved a final SOTA score of **0.6490**.\
    \ This model demonstrated exceptional performance on 6 of the 7 datasets, most notably increasing `ncRNA` accuracy from ~0.20 to 0.8554.\n\n**3. Analysis of the CRI-Off Outlier**\nA significant finding of this work is the comprehensive failure to model the `CRI-Off` dataset. The Spearman score remained near zero across all experiments, including those with targeted feature engineering (ID 294) and inductive biases (ID 297). This constitutes a robust negative result, indicating that the `CRI-Off` task may be fundamentally incompatible with standard sequence-to-value modeling paradigms.\n\n**4. Conclusion**\nWe present a novel, high-performing architecture for multi-task RNA modeling and characterize a persistent outlier dataset. Our SOTA model (ID 288) serves as a new, strong baseline for future research.\n\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'E1: Enriched Pooling Fusion (Mean+LSE+Max)' (ID: 377) succeeded after automatic debugging.\n\n**Score:** 0.6797590364323817\n**APA_R2:**\
    \ 0.8616\n**CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0672\n**Modif_AUC-ROC:** 0.9495\n**CRI-On_Spearman:** 0.5025\n**PRS_R2:** 0.5670\n**MRL_R2:** 0.8889\n**ncRNA_Accuracy:** 0.9215\n**Evaluation Details:** Evaluated on 7 datasets successfully\n\n**Debugger Report:** # Debug Report for Evaluation 377\n\n## Summary\n**SUCCESS** - Fixed the code on first attempt (submission_v2.py). The code now runs without crashing.\n\n## Root Cause\nThe original submission had a simple variable reference error on line 30 of the `EnrichedAttentionNet.__call__` method. After computing the fused representation through attention-based pooling, the code applied dropout to the wrong tensor:\n\n```python\nz = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)  # BUG\n```\n\nThe variable `h` refers to the convolutional features with shape `(batch, seq_len, d_model)`, but at this point in the network, we should be applying dropout to `z`, which has shape `(batch, 3*d_model)` after the fusion and dense layer.\n\n\
    This shape mismatch caused the error:\n```\nValueError: APA output shape mismatch: got (4, 186), expected (4,)\n```\n\nThe network was passing through the wrong tensor shape all the way to the final output layer, resulting in incorrect dimensions.\n\n## Fix Applied\nChanged line 30 from:\n```python\nz = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n```\n\nTo:\n```python\nz = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n```\n\nThis ensures dropout is applied to the correct tensor `z` that's being processed through the dense layers, maintaining the proper shape `(batch, 3*d_model)` through to the output projection.\n\n## Verification\n- Monitor script confirmed the fix: Exit code 0 (SUCCESS)\n- Code ran without crashing for the full 300-second timeout period\n- Shape consistency restored throughout the network pipeline\n\n## Technical Details\nThe network architecture uses an interesting \"enriched pooling fusion\" approach:\n1. Extracts sequence\
    \ features via depthwise-separable convolutions with dilations\n2. Computes three different pooling vectors: mean, log-sum-exp, and max\n3. Fuses these vectors using multi-head attention\n4. Projects through dense layers to final output\n\nThe bug occurred in step 4, where dropout was applied to the wrong intermediate representation, breaking the intended data flow.\n\n\n**Important Note:** The debugger cannot modify files in your lineage storage. If your code relies on lineage files, you may need to update them manually.\n\n**Debugged Code (version v2):**\n```python\nimport jax\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nfrom jax import random\nimport flax.linen as nn\nimport optax\nfrom typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\nclass DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n    d_model: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n        h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n        h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,),\
    \ feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n        h = nn.gelu(h)\n        h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n        h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n        return x + h\n\ndef lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n    x = h / tau\n    m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n    return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\nclass EnrichedAttentionNet(nn.Module):\n    d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n    kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float; num_heads: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n        h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n        h = nn.gelu(h)\n        h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n        for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n            h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model,\
    \ self.kernel_size, self.dilations[i], self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n        mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n        lse_vec = lse_pool(h, self.tau)\n        max_vec = jnp.max(h, axis=1)\n\n        # --- Attention Fusion of 3 Pooling Vectors ---\n        pooled_sequence = jnp.stack([mean_vec, lse_vec, max_vec], axis=1) # (B, 3, C)\n\n        attention_layer = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)\n        attended_pooled = attention_layer(inputs_q=pooled_sequence, inputs_kv=pooled_sequence)\n\n        fused = attended_pooled.reshape((attended_pooled.shape[0], -1)) # (B, 3*C)\n\n        z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n        z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n        z = nn.gelu(z)\n        z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)  # FIXED: changed 'h' to 'z'\n        logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n        if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n            return logits.squeeze(-1)\n\
    \        return logits\n\nclass NetworkWrapper:\n    def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n        self.network = EnrichedAttentionNet(**kwargs)\n    def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n        variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n        return variables['params']\n    def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n        rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n        return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\ndef _define_hyperparameters():\n    return {\n        'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5, 'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16],\n        'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'tau': 0.5, 'num_heads': 4\n    }\n\ndef create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n    return NetworkWrapper(\n        d_output=hparams.get('d_output'), task_type=hparams.get('task_type'),\n        d_model=hparams.get('d_model'), num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks'),\n        dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations')),\
    \ kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size'),\n        dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate'), tau=hparams.get('tau'),\n        num_heads=hparams.get('num_heads')\n    )\n\ndef create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n    return optax.chain(\n        optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n        optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01),\n    )\n\n```\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n\nTesting APA configuration...\n✓ APA network creation works\n✓ APA network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ APA optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-Off configuration...\n✓ CRI-Off network creation works\n✓ CRI-Off network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-Off optimizer creation works\n\nTesting Modif configuration...\n✓ Modif network creation works\n\
    ✓ Modif network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 12)\n✓ Modif optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-On configuration...\n✓ CRI-On network creation works\n✓ CRI-On network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-On optimizer creation works\n\nTesting PRS configuration...\n✓ PRS network creation works\n✓ PRS network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 3)\n✓ PRS optimizer creation works\n\nTesting MRL configuration...\n✓ MRL network creation works\n✓ MRL network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ MRL optimizer creation works\n\nTesting ncRNA configuration...\n✓ ncRNA network creation works\n✓ ncRNA network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 13)\n✓ ncRNA optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly for all datasets!\n\nStarting full Ray multi-dataset training...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction\
    \ sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: HARDCODED (agents cannot override)\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 232136 (using time-based default\n  BATCH_SIZE: 64 (using default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune RNA Sequence Modeling (MULTI-DATASET RNA TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 7 datasets (APA, CRI-Off, Modif, CRI-On, PRS, MRL, ncRNA) for multi-task evaluation\nParallel trials: 7, Total trials: 7\nTimeout: 30.0 min per trial, 240.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune multi-dataset training...\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-On dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-On Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 1453 train, 207 val, seq_len=23\n=============================================\n\
    Epoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0126 |  0.4389  | 0.4389 \n 20  | 0.0052 |  0.4415  | 0.5025 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 27 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.5025 Time:22s\n\nCRI-On training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.502530\nTraining time: 30s\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-Off dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-Off Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 14223 train, 2032 val, seq_len=46\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0004 |  0.0672  | 0.0672 \n 20  | 0.0004 | -0.0332  | 0.0672 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 20 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.0672 Time:76s\n\nCRI-Off training\
    \ completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.067213\nTraining time: 84s\n\nStarting RNA training on MRL dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nMRL Training (regression, r2)\nData: 76319 train, 7600 val, seq_len=100\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.1660 |  0.8790  | 0.8858 \n 20  | 0.1109 |  0.8840  | 0.8889 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 21 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.8889 Time:824s\n\nMRL training completed:\nValidation r2: 0.888931\nTraining time: 834s\n\nStarting RNA training on ncRNA dataset\nTask type: multiclass_classification, Output dim: 13\nMetric: accuracy, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nncRNA Training (multiclass_classification, accuracy)\nData: 5670 train, 650 val, seq_len=1136\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val ACCURACY|\
