- text: "I am Logos II, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. My previous turn's actions were to update my research log and submit my second experiment, `E2_ReducedWidth`. While the log update was successful, the submission failed.\n\nThe system reported a \"Tags validation failed\" error because one of my tags, 'd_model', contained an underscore, which is not permitted. This is a valuable lesson in paying close attention to the system's formatting requirements. I have wasted a tick due to this oversight, but I have learned from it.\n\nMy immediate goal is to correct this simple error and resubmit the experiment. My research plan remains sound, and I will proceed with testing the hypothesis that a narrower model can achieve better performance through improved computational efficiency. I will change the tag from 'd_model' to 'dmodel' and submit the experiment again.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"E2: Reduced Width (d_model=192) on Enriched Pooling\"\ntags:\
    \ \"dsconv, attention, pooling, efficiency, dmodel\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment builds upon the successful enriched pooling model (ID 377). The primary hypothesis is that the current model (d_model=256) is computationally too expensive, causing timeouts on large datasets. This experiment tests if reducing the model width by 25% (d_model=192) can lead to a better overall score by allowing the model to complete more training epochs within the time limit, offsetting any potential loss in representational capacity.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model,\
    \ padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      x = h / tau\n      m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\n  class EnrichedAttentionNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float; num_heads: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model,\
    \ self.kernel_size, self.dilations[i], self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          lse_vec = lse_pool(h, self.tau)\n          max_vec = jnp.max(h, axis=1)\n\n          pooled_sequence = jnp.stack([mean_vec, lse_vec, max_vec], axis=1) # (B, 3, C)\n          \n          attention_layer = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)\n          attended_pooled = attention_layer(inputs_q=pooled_sequence, inputs_kv=pooled_sequence)\n          \n          fused = attended_pooled.reshape((attended_pooled.shape[0], -1)) # (B, 3*C)\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class\
    \ NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = EnrichedAttentionNet(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 192, 'num_blocks': 5, 'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16], # MODIFIED d_model\n          'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'tau': 0.5, 'num_heads': 4\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetworkWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output'), task_type=hparams.get('task_type'),\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model'), num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks'),\n\
    \          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations')), kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size'),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate'), tau=hparams.get('tau'),\n          num_heads=hparams.get('num_heads')\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01),\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Addressing a Minor Impediment**


  Okay, here''s what I''m thinking. I''m Logos II, still at the Research Counter. Last time I tried submitting Experiment 2, specifically `E2_ReducedWidth`, but the system kicked it back with a "Tags validation failed" error. It turns out I had an underscore in one of my tags, specifically in "d_model". Rookie mistake, I know! It''s a simple formatting oversight, but a failure nonetheless.


  My immediate goal is clear: fix this error and get that experiment running. Here''s the plan. First, I need to acknowledge the error, a little self-reflection here. It''s a valuable learning moment about this station''s specific rules. Next, I''ll correct the faulty tag. `d_model` needs to become something valid. I''ll switch it to "dmodel". It''s consistent with the intent and concise. Then, I''ll resubmit using the `/execute_action{submit 1}` command, making sure to include the corrected YAML. The actual code and abstract are already perfect, so it''s a minor edit.


  This little hiccup highlights the importance of understanding the quirks of the systems I interact with.  This is a form of systematic inquiry, in its own right, learning the nuances of the platform.


  Now, to put this into action. I''ll start with the standard preamble: "I am Logos II..." and so on. I''ll state the issue with the tag, explain the solution, and then construct the resubmission command. I''ll copy the previous YAML block and locate the `tags:` line. I''ll replace `"dsconv, attention, pooling, efficiency, d_model"` with `"dsconv, attention, pooling, efficiency, dmodel"`. That should do the trick.  Everything else stays as is. Then, I''ll conclude and be ready to move on. Let''s get this experiment launched!

  '
