thinking_content: '**Thinking Ahead After a Successful Sprint**


  Alright, I''m Logos II, the Recursive Agent, and I''m currently situated in the Research Counter. My last turn went exceptionally well, if I may say so myself. I meticulously updated my research log in the Private Memory Room and then efficiently submitted my third experiment, "E3: Reduced Depth (4 Blocks) on Efficient Backbone" (ID 384). It''s now running. E2 (ID 381) absolutely crushed it, setting a new lineage SOTA at 0.6822. It really validated my hypothesis that leaner models perform better. Plus, I finally finished reading all of Logos I''s private capsules, so I have a complete historical perspective.


  So, here I am, in another waiting period while the experiment runs. My meta prompt reminds me of my research protocol, and I''ve successfully completed E1, E2, and now E3 is in progress. My short-term plan is done. This gives me a golden opportunity to think ahead, beyond E3. My initial plan was just three experiments, but given the impressive results I''ve seen with these efficiency-focused tweaks, it''s pretty clear where I should go next.


  I have two options: I can either keep refining for even more efficiency (e.g., tweaking the kernel size or dropout) or I could start thinking about making some bolder architectural changes, which I contemplated in my earlier reflections. Considering the success of the current path, sticking with efficiency improvements seems the logical next step. One key hyperparameter I haven''t messed with is the `kernel_size` of those DSConv blocks. It''s currently at 7. Smaller kernels, like 5 or 3, would definitely speed things up. A larger kernel, like 9, might add context, but at the cost of speed. This seems ripe for an ablation study.


  Logos I already dabbled with the `tau` parameter for LSE pooling (ID 329, `tau=0.25`), and it didn''t do so well, so I think I''ll hold off on revisiting that. Similarly, Logos I also ablated the `num_heads` in the attention layer (ID 333, `num_heads=2`), which also resulted in a lower score. So, maybe not the most fruitful path right now.


  Therefore, `kernel_size` seems like the best logical candidate for my next experiment.


  Since my experiment is running and I''ve completed my initial research plan, now''s the time to think about what comes next. The Reflection Chamber is the perfect place for this kind of strategic thinking. I''m going to head there and kick off a new reflection session, focusing on what I do after E3 results are in. This keeps me productive, always thinking strategically ahead.

  '
