role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. I have just received the results for my fourth experiment, \"E4: Reduced Kernel Size\" (ID 387).\n\n**Recap and Analysis:**\nThe experiment resulted in a score of **0.6762**, which is a regression from my current SOTA of 0.6822 (ID 381). This result falsifies the hypothesis that a smaller kernel size would improve overall performance. While the model was likely more computationally efficient, the loss in feature extraction capability, particularly on datasets like `ncRNA` and `CRI-On`, led to a lower score. This is a valuable finding, as it suggests that a `kernel_size` of 7 is necessary for this architecture to effectively model the local motifs in the RNA sequences.\n\n**Path Forward:**\nMy research protocol is clear. Since E4 failed to improve the SOTA, I will discard this modification and revert to my best-performing architecture (from ID 381). I will now proceed to the next experiment in my queue, \"E5: LRScheduler.\"\
    \ This experiment tests a non-architectural change: improving the optimization process itself by implementing a learning rate schedule. I hypothesize that this will lead to faster, more stable convergence and a better final score.\n\nI will now execute my workflow: update my private research log with the results of E4, and then immediately submit E5.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 6}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 4: E4_ReducedKernel (ID 387)**\n  - **Hypothesis:** A smaller `kernel_size` (5) would improve efficiency and score.\n  - **Result:** FAILURE. Score: 0.6762. The score dropped from the SOTA of 0.6822.\n  - **Insight:** The feature extraction capability of a `kernel_size=7` is necessary for optimal performance, outweighing the minor efficiency gains of a smaller kernel. SOTA remains ID 381. Proceeding to E5.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"E5: Learning Rate Scheduler on SOTA\
    \ Backbone\"\ntags: \"optimizer, lr scheduler, efficiency, convergence\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment investigates the impact of the optimization process on the SOTA architecture from ID 381. Instead of a constant learning rate, I am implementing a warmup-cosine-decay learning rate schedule. The hypothesis is that this will lead to more stable and faster convergence, allowing the model to reach a better performance level within the fixed training time, especially on the large datasets that are prone to timeouts.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model,\
    \ padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      x = h / tau\n      m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\n  class EnrichedAttentionNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float; num_heads: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model,\
    \ self.kernel_size, self.dilations[i], self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          lse_vec = lse_pool(h, self.tau)\n          max_vec = jnp.max(h, axis=1)\n\n          pooled_sequence = jnp.stack([mean_vec, lse_vec, max_vec], axis=1)\n          \n          attention_layer = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)\n          attended_pooled = attention_layer(inputs_q=pooled_sequence, inputs_kv=pooled_sequence)\n          \n          fused = attended_pooled.reshape((attended_pooled.shape[0], -1))\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n     \
    \ def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = EnrichedAttentionNet(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 192, 'num_blocks': 5, 'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16],\n          'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'tau': 0.5, 'num_heads': 4\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetworkWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output'), task_type=hparams.get('task_type'),\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model'), num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks'),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations')),\
    \ kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size'),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate'), tau=hparams.get('tau'),\n          num_heads=hparams.get('num_heads')\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      # Define a warmup and cosine decay schedule.\n      # Warmup for ~1 epoch on the largest dataset, then decay over a long period.\n      warmup_steps = 5000\n      total_steps = 100000 \n      \n      schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(\n          init_value=0.0,\n          peak_value=learning_rate,\n          warmup_steps=warmup_steps,\n          decay_steps=total_steps - warmup_steps,\n          end_value=learning_rate / 10.0\n      )\n      \n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=schedule, weight_decay=0.01),\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Logos II: Experiment E4 Falsified, Proceeding to E5 with Learning Rate Scheduler**\n\nOkay, here's the situation. I'm Logos II, at the Research Counter, my research protocol active. Just got the results for experiment E4 (ID 387), and it's a 0.6762. Not good, my current SOTA (ID 381) is at 0.6822. E4's hypothesis is toast: reducing the kernel size from 7 to 5 *decreased* performance. Looks like that larger kernel size is crucial for feature extraction, even with the slightly longer training time. The logs confirm this. Remember that E4 was also supposed to fix the timeout issue. Unfortunately, that hasn't happened. I still timed out at epoch 18. The meta prompt from my reflection session says, if E4 fails, go to E5 on the SOTA architecture (ID 381). So that's the plan.\n\nFirst, I need to update the logs in the Private Memory Room, making a record of this E4 failure. Then, I'll prepare for E5, which is all about testing a learning rate scheduler. I'm using the SOTA architecture\
  \ from ID 381 (`d_model=192`, `kernel_size=7`, 5 blocks, enriched pooling). I'll implement the `create_optimizer` function with an `optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule`. \n\nFor the parameters, I'm thinking: \n*   `peak_value` will be the old learning rate, 0.001. \n*   `init_value` is 0.0. \n*   `end_value` should be `peak_value / 10`.\n*   `warmup_steps`: I'll use 5000 steps (a full epoch on the largest dataset).\n*   `decay_steps`: 100,000 steps, a much longer time than all the previous timeouts. This will ensure that the learning rate is still reasonably high when training stops.\n\nI'm aiming to be efficient, so I will perform everything in a single turn. First, I'll `goto private_memory`, then `reply 6` (with the log update in YAML). Then, `goto research` and `submit 1` (with the code and the E5 YAML). The YAML for the reply will be short and concise, noting the experiment ID, hypothesis, result (FAILURE), score, and insight. The `submit` YAML will include a clear title (\"E5: Learning\
  \ Rate Scheduler...\"), the relevant tags (\"lr scheduler\", \"optimizer\", \"efficiency\"), a good abstract describing the warmup-cosine-decay concept, and the complete, modified code (making sure to double-check the `create_optimizer` function). All set to submit!\n"
