tick: 268
role: model
parts:
- text: "Having conceptualized the **Learnable Query Attention Pooling** mechanism, I will now focus on the specific implementation details and potential challenges. A solid plan here will prevent bugs and wasted ticks.\n\n**Implementation in Flax/JAX:**\n\nThe core of the change will be in the `EnrichedAttentionNet` class, which I will rename to `QueryAttentionNet`.\n\n1.  **Defining the Learnable Query:** The `q_vec` needs to be defined as a learnable parameter of the module. This is done within the `@nn.compact` method using `self.param`.\n\n    ```python\n    class QueryAttentionNet(nn.Module):\n        # ... other attributes ...\n        @nn.compact\n        def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n            # ... DSConv backbone ...\n            # h has shape [B, SeqLen, C]\n\n            # Define the learnable query vector.\n            # Shape: [1, C]\n            query_vec = self.param(\n                'query_vec',\n                nn.initializers.lecun_normal(),\n       \
    \         (1, self.d_model)\n            )\n\n            # Broadcast the query across the batch dimension.\n            # Shape: [B, 1, C]\n            queries = jnp.tile(query_vec, (x.shape[0], 1, 1))\n\n            # ... attention operation ...\n    ```\n\n2.  **Configuring the Attention Layer:** I will use the same `nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention` layer. The key is to pass the correct tensors to `inputs_q` and `inputs_kv`.\n\n    ```python\n    # ... inside __call__ method ...\n    attention_layer = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)\n    \n    # Query is the learnable vector, Keys/Values are the conv features.\n    # attended_output shape: [B, 1, C]\n    attended_output = attention_layer(inputs_q=queries, inputs_kv=h)\n    \n    # Flatten the output for the dense layers.\n    # fused shape: [B, C]\n    fused = attended_output.reshape((attended_output.shape[0], -1))\n\n    # ... final projection layers ...\n    ```\n\n**Hyperparameter Considerations:**\n*\
    \   `num_heads`: The number of attention heads in the `MultiHeadDotProductAttention` layer is an important hyperparameter. The previous model used 4. I will start with 4 for consistency and to isolate the impact of the new mechanism itself. If the experiment is successful, the number of heads will be a good candidate for a follow-up ablation study.\n*   `d_model`: I will use `d_model=192`, as this was proven to be the most efficient and effective width in my SOTA model (ID 381). This new pooling mechanism should be more computationally efficient than the old one (one attention operation instead of three pooling ops + one attention op), so there should be no risk of increased timeouts.\n\n**Potential Risks and Mitigations:**\n*   **Initialization:** The initialization of `q_vec` could be important. I have chosen `lecun_normal()`, a standard initializer. If training is unstable, I could try others like `xavier_uniform()`.\n*   **Single Query Limitation:** A single query vector might not\
    \ be expressive enough to capture all the different types of information needed across the seven diverse tasks. If the experiment yields a mixed result (e.g., improves on some tasks, hurts others), a follow-up experiment could involve using multiple learnable query vectors (e.g., a `[4, C]` parameter, resulting in 4 summary vectors) to see if that provides more flexibility.\n\nThis detailed implementation plan gives me high confidence that I can code this new architecture correctly and efficiently."
