tick: 269
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Reflection Chamber. My multi-tick reflection session is now complete.\n\n**Recap and Analysis:**\nThe reflection session was highly productive. I analyzed the failure of my most recent experiment, E5 (LR Scheduler), which scored 0.6650, confirming that I have likely exhausted the potential for simple tuning of the current architecture. This has prompted a strategic shift. I have now formulated a new research arc (Phase 3) that moves beyond incremental changes and introduces a novel architectural concept: **Learnable Query Attention Pooling**. This new mechanism is designed to directly address the primary theoretical weakness of the current model—the information bottleneck at the pooling layer.\n\n**Path Forward:**\nMy next actions will follow my established workflow. I will first update my meta prompt to reflect the new research plan. Then, I will document the failure of E5 in my research log. Finally, I will proceed to the Research\
    \ Counter to submit my sixth experiment, `E6_QueryAttentionPooling`, which will be the first test of my new architectural idea.\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Logos II Research Protocol**\n  - **Goal:** Surpass lineage SOTA (0.6822) with novel architecture.\n  - **Current SOTA:** 0.6822 (ID 381, Efficient DSConv + Enriched Pooling)\n  - **Experiment Queue (Phase 3):**\n    1. **[NEXT] E6_QueryAttentionPooling:** Test novel pooling mechanism.\n    2. **[WAITING] E7_MultiQueryAttention:** Contingent on E6 success.\n  - **Workflow:** On result -> update log -> prep next experiment -> submit.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 6}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 5: E5_LRScheduler (ID 389)**\n  - **Hypothesis:** A learning rate scheduler would improve convergence and score.\n  - **Result:** FAILURE. Score: 0.6650. The scheduler led to much slower convergence, harming performance on timeout-prone datasets.\n  - **Insight:** The\
    \ aggressive, constant learning rate is superior for this architecture. This concludes the incremental tuning phase. A novel architectural approach is now needed. Proceeding to E6.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"E6: Learnable Query Attention Pooling\"\ntags: \"attention, pooling, query attention, bottleneck\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment introduces a novel pooling mechanism to address the information bottleneck of previous models. Instead of using pre-defined global pooling functions (mean, max, LSE), this architecture employs a single, learnable query vector. This vector attends to the entire convolutional feature map, allowing the model to learn what positional information is most salient for the task and create a contextually-aware summary vector. This is a test of a new architectural paradigm for this task.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import\
    \ optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class QueryAttentionNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; num_heads: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, self.dilations[i], self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # --- Learnable Query Attention Pooling ---\n          # h shape: [B, SeqLen, C]\n          query_vec = self.param('query_vec', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (1, self.d_model))\n          queries = jnp.tile(query_vec, (x.shape[0], 1, 1)) # Shape: [B, 1, C]\n\n          attention_layer = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)\n          attended_output = attention_layer(inputs_q=queries, inputs_kv=h) # Shape: [B, 1, C]\n          \n          fused = attended_output.reshape((attended_output.shape[0], -1)) # Shape: [B, C]\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output,\
    \ name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = QueryAttentionNet(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 192, 'num_blocks': 5, 'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16],\n          'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'num_heads': 4\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetworkWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output'),\
    \ task_type=hparams.get('task_type'),\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model'), num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks'),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations')), kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size'),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate'), num_heads=hparams.get('num_heads')\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01),\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Current State and Next Steps: E6 - Learnable Query Attention Pooling**


  Okay, so I''m back in the Reflection Chamber after that 5-tick session. It was intense, but productive. The E5 experiment, with that LR scheduler, tanked – down to 0.6650 from my SOTA of 0.6822. I''ve hit a wall with just hyperparameter tweaks on the inherited architecture. Time for something new. I''m moving into Phase 3 with a novel idea: Learnable Query Attention Pooling.


  My meta prompt and experimental queue are outdated, still showing E4 and E5. My SOTA remains at ID 381, with that score. My next move is clear: submit E6.


  Here''s my plan: Update, Log, Submit. Standard operating procedure, but critical for progress. I''ll execute this in one swift turn.


  **Step 1:** Update the Meta Prompt. I need to get the new experiment in there, E6, and reflect this new research phase.


  **Step 2:** Log the E5 failure. It''s crucial to document this in private memory capsule #6.  Gotta keep a clean record.


  **Step 3:** Submit E6! I''ve already designed this QueryAttentionNet architecture in detail. Time to execute. I''ll need to go to the Research Counter with the new code.


  Now for the actions.  I''ll get right to it.


  ```

  /execute_action{meta} with updated YAML reflecting Phase 3 and E6 as the next experiment.

  /execute_action{goto private_memory}.

  /execute_action{reply 6} with the YAML for the E5 log entry, including the details of the score and my analysis of the LR scheduler failure.

  /execute_action{goto research}.

  /execute_action{submit 1} with the YAML for E6 and the code for QueryAttentionNet.

  ```


  The E6 code is ready. I''ll base it on my SOTA, ID 381. Rename the network class, rip out the pooling, add `self.param(''query_vec'', ...)` and the MultiHead Attention based on that vector.  I''ll tile the `query_vec` to match the batch size. Reuse the `d_model=192`, `num_blocks=5`, and `kernel_size=7` hyperparameters from the SOTA. The optimizer will go back to the constant LR.  And the abstract and title of the submission? They''ll clearly highlight this new learnable attention mechanism.


  This is a good, solid plan. Let''s get E6 running!

  '
