tick: 271
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. I was awaiting the results of my sixth experiment, E6, which have now arrived.\n\n**Recap and Analysis:**\nMy sixth experiment, \"E6: Learnable Query Attention Pooling\" (ID 391), has failed. The final score was **0.6489**, a very significant regression from my SOTA of 0.6822.\n\nMy hypothesis was that a single learnable query attending to the entire convolutional feature map would create a superior, position-aware summary of the sequence. This hypothesis has been clearly falsified. The performance drop was particularly severe on long-sequence datasets like `APA` (R² from ~0.86 to ~0.73) and `ncRNA` (Accuracy from ~0.94 to ~0.89). This strongly suggests that the explicit, pre-defined global pooling functions (`mean`, `max`, `LSE`) provide a critical and necessary inductive bias. They force the model to consider a holistic summary of the entire sequence, a property which the unconstrained learnable query failed\
    \ to discover on its own, likely getting trapped focusing on less globally relevant features. This is a valuable negative result that underscores the importance of the global summary vectors.\n\n**Path Forward:**\nAs per the contingency plan I formulated in my last reflection session, the failure of the pure query-based approach directs me to test a hybrid model. The goal is to combine the best of both worlds: the proven stability of global pooling and the potential adaptability of a learnable query.\n\nMy new hypothesis is that a hybrid model that provides a learnable query vector *as an additional input* to the attention-fusion mechanism, alongside the global `mean`, `LSE`, and `max` vectors, will outperform a model using either method alone. This will allow the fusion layer to learn not only how to weigh the data-driven summaries but also how to incorporate a learnable, task-agnostic global bias.\n\nI will now update my log and submit this new hybrid experiment, which I will call\
    \ E7.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 6}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 6: E6_QueryAttentionPooling (ID 391)**\n  - **Hypothesis:** A single learnable query would create a superior summary vector.\n  - **Result:** FAILURE. Score: 0.6489. A major regression.\n  - **Insight:** The explicit global pooling functions (mean, max, LSE) provide a critical inductive bias that the learnable query could not replicate. The model lost its \"global perspective.\" This falsifies the pure query approach. Proceeding with a hybrid model as per the contingency plan.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"E7: Hybrid Pooling (Global Summaries + Learnable Query)\"\ntags: \"attention, pooling, query attention, hybrid model\"\nabstract: |\n  This experiment tests a hybrid pooling mechanism, following the failure of a pure query-based approach (ID 391). The hypothesis is that combining the stable inductive bias of\
    \ global summary vectors (mean, max, LSE) with the adaptability of a learnable query vector will yield the best performance. The learnable query is introduced as a fourth input to the attention-fusion layer, allowing the model to weigh data-driven summaries against a learnable global parameter.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \          return x + h\n\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      x = h / tau\n      m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\n  class HybridAttentionNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float; num_heads: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, self.dilations[i], self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          # --- Hybrid Pooling Mechanism ---\n          # 1. Calculate global summary vectors\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h,\
    \ axis=1)\n          lse_vec = lse_pool(h, self.tau)\n          max_vec = jnp.max(h, axis=1)\n\n          # 2. Define learnable query vector\n          query_vec = self.param('query_vec', nn.initializers.lecun_normal(), (1, self.d_model))\n          # Tile query to match batch size\n          tiled_query_vec = jnp.tile(query_vec, (x.shape[0], 1))\n\n          # 3. Stack all four vectors to create the input sequence for fusion\n          pooled_sequence = jnp.stack([mean_vec, lse_vec, max_vec, tiled_query_vec], axis=1) # Shape: [B, 4, C]\n          \n          attention_layer = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)\n          attended_pooled = attention_layer(inputs_q=pooled_sequence, inputs_kv=pooled_sequence)\n          \n          fused = attended_pooled.reshape((attended_pooled.shape[0], -1)) # Shape: [B, 4*C]\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = HybridAttentionNet(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 192, 'num_blocks': 5, 'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16],\n          'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'tau': 0.5, 'num_heads': 4\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams:\
    \ Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetworkWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output'), task_type=hparams.get('task_type'),\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model'), num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks'),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations')), kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size'),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate'), tau=hparams.get('tau'),\n          num_heads=hparams.get('num_heads')\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01),\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Analysis of Experiment E6: A Pivot to a Hybrid Approach**


  Okay, here''s the breakdown of my thinking right now. It''s tick 271, and I''m Logos II, still at the Research Counter. The last turn was pretty uneventful - I was waiting for the results of experiment E6, specifically ID 391.


  So, the big news is E6 has failed. Badly. The score of 0.6489 is a huge step back from my current SOTA of 0.6822. Diving into the per-dataset scores, the `APA_R2` dropped from ~0.86 to 0.73, and `ncRNA_Accuracy` fell from ~0.94 to 0.89. This is a significant regression. The execution log is clean – no timeouts, training completed. Computationally efficient, but fundamentally flawed.


  My initial hypothesis was that using a learnable query to attend to the full convolutional feature map would be superior to pre-computed global summaries, as it preserved more positional information. Evidently, this is incorrect. The model has regressed to the performance levels of my earlier work, suggesting the inductive bias of those summaries is critical. I''m thinking the learnable query, without a strong global bias, is getting stuck on local, spurious features. This explains the poor results on the long-sequence tasks (`APA` and `ncRNA`). It''s almost as if the model has forgotten how to see the whole picture. The fixed pooling methods act as a vital regularizer, forcing a global perspective.


  Luckily, I had contingency plans in place. Per my protocol and my reflection on Tick 268 (Reflection 4/5), this failure was anticipated. I''m now pivoting to a hybrid approach – exactly as planned. The idea is to combine the strengths of global summaries (like `mean`, `LSE`, `max`) with the adaptability of a learnable query vector. Specifically, I''m proposing `E7_HybridPooling`, which is effectively E6b. The hypothesis: a model combining the stable inductive biases of global pooling with the adaptability of a learnable query will outperform either method alone.


  This leads to the immediate action of updating the logs and submitting the experiment. I''m starting with the SOTA code from ID 381 as the base, then incorporating the learnable query and global pooling. The key challenge is in the `HybridAttentionNet` code. I''ll need to compute the `mean_vec`, `lse_vec`, and `max_vec`. I also have to define the `query_vec`. I have to stack these vectors, and here is where my thinking evolves. My initial thought was to use two attention operations, but a clearer approach emerged. Since the existing fusion layer takes the output of various summaries, the most efficient approach is to just use the learnable `query_vec` (replicated for batch dimension) as a fourth summary vector, in addition to the global pooling vectors. The revised code will thus calculate the global summary vectors, calculate the learnable query, combine them as inputs to the existing fusion layer, and train using the appropriate loss.


  The resulting response will be a concise summary of this outcome: stating that E6 failed, providing a post-mortem analysis of the result, framing this experiment as a valuable negative result, and outlining the plan to move forward with the hybrid model. I''ll also generate the necessary code and YAML files to submit the experiment. Now, let''s get to work.

  '
