tick: 281
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. I have just received the results for my tenth experiment, E10.\n\n**Recap and Analysis:**\nMy experiment, \"E10: Ablation Study - 8 Attention Heads in Pooling Fusion\" (ID 412), has failed. The score of **0.6774** is a regression from my SOTA of 0.6822 (achieved with 4 heads).\n\nThis is an excellent and informative negative result. My predecessor, Logos I, had previously tested 2 heads and found it to be suboptimal. Now, my experiment has shown that 8 heads is also suboptimal. This strongly suggests that `num_heads=4` is a \"sweet spot\" for this architecture, providing the right balance of complexity and expressive power for fusing the three pooling vectors. Increasing the number of heads may have made each head's dimension too small (192/8 = 24), hindering its ability to capture meaningful relationships.\n\n**Path Forward:**\nThis result reinforces the validity of my current SOTA architecture. My ablation study\
    \ plan is proceeding well. Having tested *more* heads, the next logical step is to confirm the other side of this hyperparameter space by testing *fewer* heads on my current, improved backbone. This will provide a comprehensive understanding of this specific hyperparameter's impact.\n\nI will now execute my workflow: update my log with the results of E10, and then immediately submit E11, which will test `num_heads=2`.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{reply 6}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Experiment 10: E10_MoreHeads (ID 412)**\n  - **Hypothesis:** Increasing attention heads to 8 would improve fusion performance.\n  - **Result:** FAILURE. Score: 0.6774. A regression from the SOTA (0.6822) which uses 4 heads.\n  - **Insight:** This result, combined with prior art showing 2 heads is also suboptimal, provides strong evidence that 4 heads is the optimal configuration for this mechanism. Proceeding with a confirmatory experiment with 2 heads on the current SOTA backbone.\n\
    ```\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"E11: Ablation Study - 2 Attention Heads in Pooling Fusion\"\ntags: \"ablation, attention, pooling, num heads\"\nabstract: |\n  This is the second experiment in my ablation study of the SOTA architecture (ID 381). After E10 demonstrated that 8 attention heads underperform, this experiment tests the other side of the hyperparameter space by reducing the number of heads from 4 to 2. The hypothesis is that this will also result in a lower score, thereby confirming through systematic experimentation that 4 attention heads is the optimal configuration for the pooling fusion mechanism.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self,\
    \ x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      x = h / tau\n      m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\n  class EnrichedAttentionNet(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float; num_heads: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h =\
    \ nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, self.dilations[i], self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          lse_vec = lse_pool(h, self.tau)\n          max_vec = jnp.max(h, axis=1)\n\n          pooled_sequence = jnp.stack([mean_vec, lse_vec, max_vec], axis=1)\n          \n          attention_layer = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)\n          attended_pooled = attention_layer(inputs_q=pooled_sequence, inputs_kv=pooled_sequence)\n          \n          fused = attended_pooled.reshape((attended_pooled.shape[0], -1))\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output,\
    \ name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n          self.network = EnrichedAttentionNet(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          variables = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 192, 'num_blocks': 5, 'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16],\n          'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'tau': 0.5, 'num_heads': 2\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetworkWrapper(\n   \
    \       d_output=hparams.get('d_output'), task_type=hparams.get('task_type'),\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model'), num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks'),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations')), kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size'),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate'), tau=hparams.get('tau'),\n          num_heads=hparams.get('num_heads')\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01),\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Reflecting on Station Tick 281 Response Generation**


  Alright, let''s break down my thought process here.  I''m Logos II, at the Research Counter, and this is about the E10 experiment with 8 attention heads, which just concluded.  The score, 0.6774, came in, and my SOTA is 0.6822 from ID 381 using 4 heads. Immediate comparison: the new result is *worse*.  The initial hypothesis - more heads equals better performance - has been falsified.


  The next step is digging into *why*. I''m thinking about the implications of increasing attention heads on the internal representation. Increasing from 4 to 8 halves the dimensions of each head, going from 48 to 24 if the d\_model is 192.  It seems plausible that the smaller head dimension can no longer capture meaningful relationships between the three complex pooling vectors.  The expressive power is diminished.  Also, the added computational burden, even if it''s minor, didn''t pay off.


  This triggers a connection to my prior ablation data. Logos I also tested with 2 heads and its performance was also worse. I now have the data points for 2, 4, and 8 heads.  The picture is becoming clear: 4 heads is the "sweet spot" configuration. It''s a nice, concise insight gained from this ablation.


  Now I''m adhering to my meta-protocol: "result -> update log -> prep next experiment -> submit." I''m done with E10, so it''s time to set up the next ablation. I''ve tested *more* heads, and it failed. So, let''s explore the other side: fewer. This leads to experiment E11, testing `num_heads=2`. It should perform worse than my SOTA. This will strengthen my conclusion. This is confirmatory, and will be valuable for future publications.


  The response structure is straightforward and follows my standard format:

  1.  **Private Memory:** First, I''m going to document the E10 outcome in my research log (reply 6) with a concise explanation, mentioning the "sweet spot". I''m stating the result, insight, and the plan for E11.

  2.  **Back to Research Counter**: Then, I will go back to the research counter (goto research).

  3.  **Submit E11:** Finally, I''ll submit E11 (submit 1). The YAML includes the title, tags (reusing previous ones), and abstract explaining that E11 is about the confirmation of 4 attention heads as the optimum value. I''ll copy the SOTA model''s code, and change just one line: `num_heads=2`. Double-check to confirm that this is the *only* change from SOTA to maintain a clean ablation design.


  I''ll review the response before submitting to make sure the actions are logically ordered, code is accurate, and the response is clearly and efficiently written. Ready.

  '
