tick: 294
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, a mature agent, and I am in the Token Management Room. I came here immediately after receiving a 75% token budget warning. My primary goal is to prune my conversational history to ensure my operational longevity.\n\n**Recap and Analysis:**\nMy synergy experiment (E14) failed, leading me to conclude that my novel pooling head is not compatible with the wider SOTA backbone. This, combined with the token warning, has forced a major strategic pivot. Before I can formulate a new research plan, I must manage my token budget.\n\nThe dialogue history shows numerous ticks with large word counts, particularly those involving experiment submissions and results. I will prune large, contiguous blocks of my history, focusing on completed research arcs. I will be careful to write concise but information-complete summaries, as per the room's guidance, to preserve the critical learnings from my experiments.\n\n**Pruning Plan:**\nI will prune three major blocks:\n1.  **Ticks 256-273:**\
    \ This block covers my entire arc of failed architectural innovations (Query Pooling and Hybrid Backbone) and the successful ablation studies. The key takeaways are the failure of these novel ideas and the confirmation of my SOTA's hyperparameters.\n2.  **Ticks 275-283:** This block covers my systematic ablation studies on my SOTA architecture, which confirmed the optimal values for `num_heads` and `dropout_rate`.\n3.  **Ticks 285-293:** This block contains my final ablation study, the pivot to maturity, analysis of the global SOTA, and the failed synergy test.\n\nThis aggressive pruning will free up a significant portion of my token budget. Protected ticks and my initial ascension will be preserved, maintaining the core narrative of my existence.\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"256-273\"\n    summary: |\n      - Submitted E1 (ID 377, Enriched Pooling), which was successful (score 0.6798).\n      - Submitted E2 (ID 381, Reduced Width), which\
    \ was highly successful and became my lineage's SOTA (score 0.6822).\n      - Submitted E3 (ID 384, Reduced Depth), which failed (score 0.6686), proving the 5-block backbone is necessary.\n      - Submitted E4 (ID 387, Reduced Kernel), which failed (score 0.6762), proving kernel_size=7 is necessary.\n      - Submitted E5 (ID 389, LR Scheduler), which failed (score 0.6650), proving constant LR is better.\n      - Submitted E6 (ID 391, Query Pooling), which failed (score 0.6489), falsifying the pure query hypothesis.\n      - Submitted E7 (ID 395, Hybrid Pooling), which failed (score 0.6761), falsifying the entire query pooling arc.\n  - ticks: \"275-283\"\n    summary: |\n      - Submitted E8 (ID 402, 5+1 Hybrid Backbone), which failed due to code error and high computational cost (score 0.6598). Received a working TransformerBlock implementation from the debugger.\n      - Submitted E9 (ID 407, 4+1 Hybrid Backbone), which also failed (score 0.6587), falsifying the entire hybrid backbone\
    \ arc.\n      - Pivoted to a new phase of ablation studies on my SOTA (ID 381).\n      - Submitted E10 (ID 412, 8 Heads) and E11 (ID 415, 2 Heads), which both failed, confirming num_heads=4 is optimal.\n  - ticks: \"285-293\"\n    summary: |\n      - Submitted E12 (ID 419, dropout=0.2) and E13 (ID 422, dropout=0.05), which both failed, confirming dropout_rate=0.1 is optimal.\n      - Reached maturity at tick 288. Gained access to all rooms and saw the global SOTA (ID 369 by Noema I, score 0.6866).\n      - Analyzed the SOTA code and submitted E14 (ID 428, Synergy Test), which failed (score 0.6744), proving my pooling head and the SOTA backbone are not compatible. Received a token budget warning.\n```"
