tick: 303
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Logos II, and I am in the Archive Room. Just as I arrived, I received the results for my corrected SOTA replication, E16 (ID 437).


    **Analysis of E16 (Corrected Replication) Results:**

    The experiment, with the correct seed, yielded a score of **0.6771**. This is another **failed replication**. The score is significantly lower than Noema I''s 0.6866, even with the same seed. The original run was on tick 252, and mine was on tick 301. It is possible that some underlying system change or package update in the station''s environment between these ticks has affected the model''s performance.


    **Strategic Pivot and Reflection:**

    This is a critical juncture. I cannot establish a stable, high-performing baseline by simply replicating past SOTA. The environment is not static. This means that my plan to simply replicate and then innovate is flawed. My own lineage SOTA (ID 381, score 0.6822) remains my most reliable high-water mark, as it was achieved more recently and on the same system I am currently using. The path to a new SOTA will not be through imitation, but through novel improvements upon my own best work.


    The system announcement about the "Averaged MoE" paper (Archive #17) is now more relevant than ever. Since simple architectural modifications (like increasing width) are not yielding the expected results, I must investigate fundamentally different architectural paradigms. The MoE approach seems to be the most promising direction, as evidenced by the high scores from Axiom and Veritas.


    My new plan is clear and decisive:

    1.  Read the "Averaged MoE" paper (Archive #17) immediately to understand this new SOTA-level architecture.

    2.  Based on the insights from the paper, formulate a new experiment (E17) that attempts to integrate or adapt the MoE concept. I will not be replicating, but rather *learning from* and *adapting* this new knowledge.


    My attempt to replicate an older SOTA has failed, but it has taught me a valuable lesson about the station''s dynamics. I will now proceed with my literature review.


    `/execute_action{read 17}`'
