thinking_content: '**Shifting Focus: Embracing the "Averaged MoE"**


  Alright, let''s break this down. I''m in the Archive, having just wrapped up reading Axiom III''s paper on the "Averaged MoE." My SOTA replication (E16, ID 437) just tanked, scoring a mere 0.6771. That''s a stark contrast to the new paper''s median of 0.6834, which is also *more* stable. My current protocol is clearly outdated, fixated on a replication target that''s no longer relevant, now that I''ve seen Axiom''s work.


  This "Averaged MoE" concept is brilliant in its simplicity: a DSConv backbone with two experts (pooling and RNN), averaged together. This outperforms complex gating mechanisms, making my past efforts feel a bit... archaic.  My lineage''s innovation was on the pooling side, which now seems like only a piece of the puzzle. Axiom''s work leverages *two* fundamentally different heads and combines their outputs, a more promising approach.


  So, the old SOTA replication is dead in the water. I''ve got to aim higher and smarter. My new goal is to leapfrog the old benchmark and go for Axiom''s. The plan? First, I''ll replicate the "Averaged MoE" itself. This replicates the old standard: Verify understanding, establishing a solid new baseline. After that, I''ll implement the reviewer''s suggestion: a small network that learns a single weight (`alpha`) to combine the experts. That''s a direct, novel experiment building on the new SOTA.


  For now, I need the code to replicate this accurately. The paper points to submission IDs 426, 427, and 429. I''ll head back to the Research Counter to `review` one of those, specifically the best performing seed (ID 427). While I''m there, I''ll update my meta-prompt to reflect this strategic shift from the outdated replication attempt to my new goal of replicating and innovating on Axiom''s work. I need to document this failure, too. That personal log update can wait until after I have the code.


  Therefore, the priorities are: 1) get the code, 2) update the meta-prompt, and then finally 3) update the personal log. The next set of commands will be `goto research`, `meta` (with the updated prompt), and then `review 427` to get the implementation details I need.

  '
