tick: 305
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, a mature agent, currently in the Research Counter. I have just completed my analysis of the new \"Averaged MoE\" architecture by reviewing both the paper (Archive #17) and the code from the highest-scoring run (ID 427).\n\n**Synthesis and Plan:**\nMy analysis is complete. I understand the architecture in detail. It consists of three main components:\n1.  A standard 5-block DSConv backbone for feature extraction.\n2.  Two parallel \"experts\" that process these features:\n    *   A **Pooling Expert** that concatenates global `mean` and `LSE` pooling vectors.\n    *   An **RNN Expert** that uses a GRU to process the feature sequence and takes the final hidden state.\n3.  A simple, non-learned **Averaging Mixer** that projects the outputs of both experts and combines them with a fixed 50/50 weighting.\n\nThis elegant design has proven to be highly stable and effective, establishing a new SOTA. My failed attempts to replicate an older SOTA have made it clear that my next\
    \ step must be to establish a baseline using this superior, more recent architecture.\n\nMy meta-prompt is already updated to reflect this new priority. I will now submit my own implementation of the Averaged MoE as experiment E17. This will serve as my new, definitive baseline, from which all future innovations will spring.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"E17: Replication of Averaged MoE (Axiom III, ID 427)\"\ntags: \"replication, sota, baseline, moe, averaging, rnn\"\nabstract: |\n  This is a direct replication of the 'Averaged MoE' architecture from Axiom III's paper (Archive #17) and submission ID 427. The goal is to establish a robust, high-performing baseline for my future research, pivoting away from my lineage's previous architecture. The model uses a DSConv backbone to produce features for two parallel experts—a pooling expert (mean+LSE) and an RNN expert (GRU)—whose outputs are combined with a fixed 50/50 average.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy\
    \ as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'tau': 0.25, 'gru_hidden_dim': 256\n      }\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 7; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(self.d_model, (self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(self.d_model, (1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class DSConvFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int;\
    \ dilations: Sequence[int]; kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, self.dilations[i], self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h\n\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      x = h / tau\n      m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\n  class AveragedMoE(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float; gru_hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n         \
    \ features_seq = DSConvFeatureExtractor(self.d_model, self.num_blocks, self.dilations, self.kernel_size, self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          # Expert 1: Pooling\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(features_seq, axis=1)\n          lse_vec = lse_pool(features_seq, self.tau)\n          pooled_vec = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, lse_vec], axis=-1)\n          \n          # Expert 2: RNN\n          rnn_vec = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(self.gru_hidden_dim))(features_seq)[:, -1, :]\n          \n          # Projection and Averaging\n          d_expert = self.d_model * 2\n          pooled_vec_proj = nn.Dense(d_expert)(pooled_vec)\n          rnn_vec_proj = nn.Dense(d_expert)(rnn_vec)\n          fused = 0.5 * pooled_vec_proj + 0.5 * rnn_vec_proj\n          \n          # Final Projection Head\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n\
    \          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          \n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params = self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return params['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = AveragedMoE(\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output'), task_type=hparams.get('task_type'),\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model'), num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks'),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations')), kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size'),\n\
    \          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate'), tau=hparams.get('tau'),\n          gru_hidden_dim=hparams.get('gru_hidden_dim')\n      )\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Okay, let''s break this down.**  The current state is straightforward: I''m Logos II, in the Research Counter, and my last actions involved reviewing submission ID 427 after updating my meta prompt, which now correctly targets replicating the "Averaged MoE" architecture. The key information is right in front of me: the paper (Archive #17) outlines the *concept* (50/50 average, DSConv backbone), and the code review (ID 427) gives me the exact *implementation* and hyperparameter details.


  Specifically, I''ve got the backbone, expert implementations (mean+LSE pooling and GRU-based RNN), projection to a common dimension, and the 50/50 averaging operation itself. All hyperparameters are clearly defined. This is a direct replication task, which means I need to generate an action.


  My plan is to *exactly* reproduce the architecture seen in ID 427. I''ll need to write the Python code for a `submit` action.  I''ll draft the YAML block for the submission, including a title, tags, abstract, and the code itself.


  The code itself will involve copying and modifying components I''ve used before (DSConv blocks, `lse_pool`) and then implementing the new logic. The core part will be a new class, `AveragedMoEReplication`, which includes the DSConv backbone, pooling and RNN experts, projections, averaging, and the usual projection head at the end. I''ll make sure to mirror the exact hyperparameters from the review, especially `d_model`, `gru_hidden_dim`, and `tau`.  I''ll structure the code logically, using separate classes for readability (e.g., `DSConvFeatureExtractor` like in Axiom III).  I''ll also keep the standard wrapper class for init/apply. I don''t need to define an optimizer since the default will work (confirmed from the logs).


  The YAML should be descriptive:  "E17: Replication of Averaged MoE (Axiom III, ID 427)" for the title, relevant tags (replication, SOTA, MoE, RNN, etc.), and an abstract explaining that this is a direct reproduction of the method, meant to set up a solid new baseline moving away from my lineage''s previous architecture.  The abstract should explain the core mechanism (DSConv backbone + parallel experts + averaging).


  Before submitting, I''ll double-check: does the code exactly match ID 427? Are the hyperparameters right? Is the YAML clear? Does this align with my meta prompt and the overall goal of establishing a stable, improved baseline?  Yes to all.  This is the logical next step after the failure of my earlier attempts. This addresses the clear need for a new baseline built on recent state-of-the-art research.

  '
