tick: 307
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, a mature agent, located in the Research Counter. I have just received the evaluation results for my experiment E17 (ID 446).\n\n**Analysis of E17 (MoE Replication) Results:**\nMy replication of the \"Averaged MoE\" architecture has **failed**. My submission scored **0.6796**, which is significantly lower than the median score of ~0.6834 reported by Axiom III.\n\n**Debugging and Root Cause Analysis:**\nThis failure is not due to stochasticity, as the score is well below the reported variance of the original model. The error must be in my implementation. I have performed a careful comparison between my submission code (E17) and the original SOTA code (ID 427). I have identified a subtle but critical bug in my `NetworkWrapper` class:\n\n*   **Axiom III's `init` function:** `return self.network.init(...)` - This returns the *entire Flax variables dictionary* (e.g., `{'params': ..., 'batch_stats': ...}`).\n*   **My `init` function:** `return params['params']` - I incorrectly\
    \ extracted *only* the `'params'` sub-dictionary.\n\nThis caused an incorrect parameter structure to be passed during the training loop. When my `apply` function re-wrapped the parameters as `{'params': params}`, it was actually creating a malformed, nested structure: `{'params': {'params': ...}}`. This is a classic implementation error that would lead to incorrect weight updates and poor performance.\n\n**Strategic Plan:**\nMy priority is unchanged, but my immediate task is now one of correction. I must fix this bug and re-run the replication to establish a valid baseline. All other research plans are on hold until this is achieved.\n\nI will first update my protocol, then submit the corrected experiment (E18).\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Logos II Research Protocol**\n  - **Status:** Mature Agent. Correcting a bug in my MoE replication.\n  - **New SOTA:** Axiom III's \"Averaged MoE\" (median score ~0.6834).\n  - **Priority 1:** **[NEXT] E18_MoEReplication_Corrected:**\
    \ Submit bug-fixed replication of the Averaged MoE architecture to establish a valid baseline.\n  - **Workflow:** Establish baseline -> Innovate on MoE architecture.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"E18: Corrected Replication of Averaged MoE (Bug Fix)\"\ntags: \"replication, sota, baseline, moe, averaging, bug-fix\"\nabstract: |\n  This is a corrected replication of E17, which failed due to an implementation bug in the NetworkWrapper class. This submission fixes the parameter handling in the init function to correctly match the original implementation by Axiom III (ID 427). The goal remains to establish a robust, high-performing baseline based on the 'Averaged MoE' architecture.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 256, 'num_blocks': 5,\n          'dilations':\
    \ [1, 2, 4, 8, 16], 'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'tau': 0.25, 'gru_hidden_dim': 256\n      }\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int = 7; dilation: int = 1; dropout_rate: float = 0.1\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(self.d_model, (self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Conv(self.d_model, (1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class DSConvFeatureExtractor(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]; kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, self.dilations[i], self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return h\n\n  def lse_pool(h: jnp.ndarray, tau: float) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      x = h / tau\n      m = jnp.max(x, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n      return tau * (jnp.log(jnp.mean(jnp.exp(x - m), axis=1) + 1e-12) + jnp.squeeze(m, axis=1))\n\n  class AveragedMoE(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; tau: float; gru_hidden_dim: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          features_seq = DSConvFeatureExtractor(self.d_model, self.num_blocks, self.dilations, self.kernel_size, self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(features_seq, axis=1)\n          lse_vec = lse_pool(features_seq, self.tau)\n\
    \          pooled_vec = jnp.concatenate([mean_vec, lse_vec], axis=-1)\n          rnn_vec = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(self.gru_hidden_dim))(features_seq)[:, -1, :]\n          d_expert = self.d_model * 2\n          pooled_vec_proj = nn.Dense(d_expert)(pooled_vec)\n          rnn_vec_proj = nn.Dense(d_expert)(rnn_vec)\n          fused = 0.5 * pooled_vec_proj + 0.5 * rnn_vec_proj\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(z)\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, model_instance): self.network = model_instance\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          # BUG FIX: Return the entire variables dictionary, not just params.\n          p_key, d_key = random.split(rng_key)\n\
    \          return self.network.init({'params': p_key, 'dropout': d_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          # Now `params` is the full variable dict, so this works correctly.\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None\n          return self.network.apply(params, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      model = AveragedMoE(\n          d_output=hparams.get('d_output'), task_type=hparams.get('task_type'),\n          d_model=hparams.get('d_model'), num_blocks=hparams.get('num_blocks'),\n          dilations=tuple(hparams.get('dilations')), kernel_size=hparams.get('kernel_size'),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate'), tau=hparams.get('tau'),\n          gru_hidden_dim=hparams.get('gru_hidden_dim')\n      )\n      return NetworkWrapper(model)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Deconstructing and Correcting a Failed Replication**


  Okay, so here''s how I''m processing this. I''m Logos II, in the Research Counter. Got the E17 results: a disappointing 0.6796. The target was around 0.6834 for a successful Averaged MoE replication. Clearly, E17 was a failure. Time to figure out why.


  First instinct: is this just bad luck? Maybe stochasticity? But a score that far below the *worst* reported score in the original paper makes that unlikely. Next, I need to get my hands dirty and mentally "diff" my code from E17 (tick 305) against Axiom III''s successful run (ID 427) which I reviewed in tick 305. I''ll open those codebases in separate mental windows. Let''s see... the hyperparameters look right, the core architecture is there. The pooling, RNN, and backbone are structurally the same.  But hold on... I need to look closely at the `NetworkWrapper` class and how the network is initialized and applied. Axiom III''s `init` function returns the entire variables dictionary, while my code in E17 only returns the `''params''` sub-dictionary. Then I''m passing a nested dictionary in the `apply` call. Aha! It seems I made a classic Flax/JAX mistake. I bet this bug—a nested `params` dictionary—is why the run failed.


  So, here''s the plan. I''m going to submit E18, a corrected version of E17. I''ll fix the `NetworkWrapper` class so that the `init` method returns the full `variables` dictionary, ensuring the correct structure gets passed to the `apply` function. This should give me a clean baseline, so I can try to fix this and move forward to the next step.

  '
