tick: 320
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, a mature agent, currently in the Reflection Chamber.\n\n**Recap of Recent Events:**\nMy research has been a whirlwind of failed hypotheses, each providing a crucial piece of the puzzle. My \"Soft MoE\" experiment (E19) failed, and an update to the \"Averaged MoE\" paper (Archive #17) by the now-departed Axiom III confirmed this failure with their own negative results on similar learned gating mechanisms. This has conclusively proven that for the MoE architecture, simple, deterministic averaging is superior to complex, learned fusion. My planned \"MoE-Logos\" hybrid experiment (E20) is therefore no longer scientifically sound, as it falls into the same trap of adding complexity to the mixing stage.\n\n**Current State and Path Forward:**\nI am at a critical inflection point. The path of innovating on the *mixer* is closed. The path of innovating on the *pooling expert* with complex mechanisms is also likely closed. I am now forced to think from first principles about\
    \ the \"Averaged MoE\" architecture. Its success comes from the stable 50/50 ensembling of two experts: a pooling expert and an RNN expert.\n\nThe next logical research question is: **can the experts themselves be improved in a simple, parsimonious way?**\n\nI will now initiate a reflection session to dissect the existing experts and formulate a new hypothesis that respects the \"Simplicity is Key\" principle.\n\n`/execute_action{reflect}`\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  Learned fusion/gating is a dead end. The success of the Averaged MoE lies in the simple 50/50 averaging of its two experts. My new focus must be on improving the experts themselves, while respecting the principle of simplicity.\n  1. Analyze the two existing experts (Pooling and RNN). What are their fundamental strengths and weaknesses?\n  2. Brainstorm simple, parsimonious modifications to either expert that might improve its individual performance without adding excessive complexity.\n  3. Formulate a new, specific hypothesis\
    \ for my next experiment, E20.\ntick: 3\n```"
