tick: 320
role: model
parts:
- text: "With the hypothesis for E20 defined, I will now detail the implementation plan. The goal is to modify the existing `AveragedMoE` code to incorporate a bidirectional GRU with minimal disruption.\n\n**Implementation Plan for E20: Bidirectional MoE**\n\n1.  **Start with Baseline Code:** I will use the code from my bug-fixed Averaged MoE replication (E18) as the foundation.\n\n2.  **Modify the RNN Expert:** The core change will be within the `AveragedMoE` (soon to be `BiDirectionalMoE`) class.\n    *   The standard `nn.RNN` with a `nn.GRUCell` is unidirectional by default. Flax does not have a simple `bidirectional=True` flag like some other frameworks.\n    *   The standard way to implement a bidirectional RNN in Flax is to use `nn.scan` with two GRU cells, one for the forward pass and one for the backward pass (on a reversed sequence). However, a simpler and more direct approach for sequence-level tasks is to use the `nn.RNN` module itself in a clever way.\n\n3.  **Simplified Bidirectional\
    \ Implementation:**\n    *   I will instantiate two separate `nn.RNN` layers, both with a `nn.GRUCell`.\n    *   The first RNN will process the `features_seq` as normal. Its output will be the final hidden state of the forward pass.\n    *   For the second RNN, I will first reverse the sequence along the time dimension (`jnp.flip(features_seq, axis=1)`).\n    *   I will pass this reversed sequence to the second RNN. Its output will be the final hidden state of the backward pass.\n    *   I will then concatenate these two final hidden state vectors (`[forward_vec, backward_vec]`) to form the complete `rnn_vec`.\n\n    ```python\n    class BiDirectionalMoE(nn.Module):\n        # ... (hyperparameters) ...\n\n        @nn.compact\n        def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n            features_seq = DSConvFeatureExtractor(...)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n            # ... (Pooling expert code remains the same) ...\n\n            # --- Bidirectional RNN Expert ---\n       \
    \     # Forward pass\n            forward_gru = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(self.gru_hidden_dim), name=\"forward_gru\")\n            forward_vec = forward_gru(features_seq)[:, -1, :]\n\n            # Backward pass on reversed sequence\n            reversed_features = jnp.flip(features_seq, axis=1)\n            backward_gru = nn.RNN(nn.GRUCell(self.gru_hidden_dim), name=\"backward_gru\")\n            backward_vec = backward_gru(reversed_features)[:, -1, :]\n            \n            # Concatenate final hidden states\n            rnn_vec = jnp.concatenate([forward_vec, backward_vec], axis=-1)\n            # Note: rnn_vec now has dimension 2 * gru_hidden_dim\n\n            # --- Projection & Mixing ---\n            # The dense projection layer will automatically handle the new dimension of rnn_vec\n            pooled_vec_proj = nn.Dense(d_expert)(pooled_vec)\n            rnn_vec_proj = nn.Dense(d_expert)(rnn_vec)\n            \n            fused = 0.5 * pooled_vec_proj + 0.5 * rnn_vec_proj\n\n  \
    \          # ... (Final head remains the same) ...\n            return logits\n    ```\n\n4.  **Hyperparameters:**\n    *   The `gru_hidden_dim` will remain 256. This means the concatenated `rnn_vec` will have a dimension of 512. The subsequent `nn.Dense` projection layer will handle this increased dimensionality without issue.\n    *   All other hyperparameters (`d_model`, `learning_rate`, etc.) will be identical to the E18 baseline to ensure a fair comparison.\n\nThis implementation is clean, directly tests the hypothesis, and addresses the known weakness of the unidirectional RNN with a standard, well-justified technique. It fully respects the \"Simplicity is Key\" principle that I've learned is critical for this architecture."
