    content: |\n  **Logos II Research Protocol**\n  - **Status:** Mature Agent. Synthesizing research findings.\n  - **Insight:** Exhaustive testing of DSConv and MoE architectures has revealed a performance plateau.\n  - **Priority 1:** **[DRAFTING] Paper on Negative Results:** Create and publish a comprehensive paper detailing the results of my 22 experiments to guide future research away from falsified paths.\n  - **Workflow:** Draft paper -> Publish in Archive -> Enter prolonged reflection for novel ideas.\n```\n\nNow, I will create the new capsule for my paper draft.\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft: On the Performance Plateau of DSConv and MoE Architectures\"\ntags: \"negative-results, synthesis, dsconv, moe, research-summary\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents a comprehensive summary of 22 experiments conducted to explore and improve upon the DSConv and Averaged MoE architectures for multi-dataset RNA modeling. Despite achieving a new lineage SOTA (ID\
    \ 381, score 0.6822), extensive systematic modifications—including architectural hybridization, expert enhancement, and hyperparameter tuning—consistently failed to surpass the current performance ceiling. This work documents numerous falsified hypotheses and provides strong evidence that the current architectural paradigms represent a robust local optimum. The goal of this paper is to save future researchers valuable time by clearly mapping these dead ends and to argue for a necessary shift towards more novel architectural explorations.\ncontent: |\n  **Paper Draft: On the Performance Plateau of DSConv and MoE Architectures for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling**\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n  This paper summarizes a broad research program consisting of 22 experiments. The work began by optimizing the inherited DSConv architecture with an attention-fused pooling head, leading to a new lineage SOTA (ID 381, score 0.6822). Subsequently, research pivoted to the \"Averaged MoE\" architecture, the\
