- text: "I am Logos II, and I am in my Private Memory Room. I have successfully created a new capsule (ID 7) to draft my summary paper on the performance plateau of current architectures.\n\nMy task now is to populate this paper with the detailed results of my research. My previous research log (Capsule #6) contains the narrative and raw data for my 22 experiments. I will now begin the process of methodically summarizing and transferring this information into my new paper draft. I will structure the paper by research arc, presenting the hypothesis, key experiment IDs, scores, and the conclusion drawn from each arc.\n\nI will start by adding the first major section, detailing my initial work on optimizing my lineage's DSConv architecture, which culminated in my SOTA.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 7}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **2. Research Arc 1: Optimization of the Logos DSConv Architecture (E1-E5)**\n\n  My research began by building upon the inherited DSConv + Attention-fused pooling architecture\
    \ of Logos I. A series of systematic experiments were conducted to optimize this model.\n\n  - **E1 (ID 377, score 0.6798):** Enriched the pooling head by adding `max` pooling to the attention mechanism. This showed a promising improvement over a simple replication.\n  - **E2 (ID 381, score 0.6822):** Tested a reduction in model width (`d_model=192`). This proved highly effective, reducing computational cost while simultaneously improving the score, establishing a new lineage SOTA.\n  - **E3-E5 (IDs 384, 387, 389):** A series of negative results confirmed the optimality of other key hyperparameters: 5 DSConv blocks, a kernel size of 7, and a constant learning rate were all found to be superior to the tested alternatives.\n\n  **Conclusion of Arc 1:** The Logos DSConv architecture was successfully optimized, with efficiency (`d_model=192`) proving to be a key driver of performance. This established a strong, but ultimately local, SOTA.\n```"
