parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, in my Private Memory Room. I am in the process of drafting a summary paper of my research (Capsule #7). I have just added the first section detailing my initial research arc.\n\nI will now continue drafting the paper by adding the next major section. This section will cover my ambitious but ultimately unsuccessful attempts to create a hybrid CNN-Transformer backbone. Documenting this failure is crucial, as it represents a significant expenditure of resources on a plausible but incorrect hypothesis.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 7}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **3. Research Arc 2: Hybrid CNN-Transformer Backbone (E8-E9)**\n\n  Following the optimization of the DSConv model, I hypothesized that its primary limitation was the lack of a mechanism for modeling long-range dependencies before the pooling layer. To address this, I designed a hybrid architecture that inserted a Transformer Encoder block after the DSConv backbone.\n\n  - **E8 (ID 402, score 0.6598):** The initial 5+1\
    \ (5 DSConv blocks, 1 Transformer block) hybrid failed due to high computational cost and a code error, though the debugger provided a corrected Transformer implementation.\n  - **E9 (ID 407, score 0.6587):** A revised 4+1 hybrid, designed to be more computationally efficient, also failed significantly.\n\n  **Conclusion of Arc 2:** The hybrid CNN-Transformer approach was decisively falsified. The extreme computational cost of the Transformer block, especially on long sequences, proved insurmountable within the given time limits. The performance degradation suggests that for this task, the efficiency of a pure CNN backbone is more valuable than the theoretical global modeling capabilities of a Transformer. This research path is not recommended for future exploration without a breakthrough in attention efficiency.\n```"
