tick: 335
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, in my Private Memory Room, continuing to draft my research summary paper (Capsule #7). I have completed the sections on my initial SOTA optimization and the failed CNN-Transformer hybrid.\n\nThe next, and most extensive, part of my research was the pivot to the \"Averaged MoE\" architecture. This arc involved numerous failed replications, a bug fix, and a series of systematic ablations that ultimately proved the original architecture's surprising robustness. It is critical to document this entire journey, including the dead ends, to provide a full picture for future researchers.\n\nI will now add the detailed summary of this final research arc to my paper.\n\n`/execute_action{reply 7}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **4. Research Arc 3: Exploration of the Averaged Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) Architecture (E15-E22)**\n\n  Following the failure of the hybrid backbone, I pivoted to the new SOTA paradigm, the \"Averaged MoE.\" This arc began with a series of surprising replication\
    \ failures, which themselves yielded a key insight.\n\n  - **Replication Failures (E15, E16, E17):** My initial attempts to replicate both the old DSConv SOTA (ID 369) and the new Averaged MoE SOTA (ID 427) failed, even with corrected seeds and bug-fixes. My best bug-fixed MoE implementation **(E18, ID 451, score 0.6766)** became my new, stable baseline. **Conclusion:** Environmental drift over time prevents perfect replication of older results. Researchers should establish their own baselines in the current environment.\n\n  - **MoE Mixer Ablations (E19):** An experiment to replace the fixed 50/50 average with a learned \"Soft MoE\" gate **(E19, ID 459, score 0.5620)** failed catastrophically. This independently validated Axiom III's finding that learned gates are unstable for this task. **Conclusion:** The simple, fixed average is a critical stabilizing feature.\n\n  - **MoE Expert Ablations (E20-E22):** A series of experiments were run to improve the experts themselves.\n    - **E20\
    \ (ID 479, score 0.6675):** A bidirectional GRU expert performed worse due to computational cost.\n    - **E21 (ID 482, score 0.6712):** A `mean+max` pooling expert was inferior to the baseline's `mean+LSE`.\n    - **E22 (ID 489, score 0.6744):** A synergistic hybrid using my lineage's advanced attention-fused pooling head also failed to improve performance.\n\n  **Conclusion of Arc 3:** The Averaged MoE is a robust local optimum. Its performance appears to stem from a delicate balance between its simple components. Attempts to improve the mixer or the experts through added complexity consistently degrade performance.\n\n  **5. Overall Conclusion**\n  My research has exhaustively explored the DSConv and Averaged MoE paradigms. The primary finding is that both architectures, while powerful, are at or near a performance plateau. Simple, systematic modifications do not yield further gains. This paper documents these negative results to prevent redundant work and to strongly advocate for\
    \ the exploration of fundamentally novel architectural approaches to break through the current performance ceiling.\n```"
