- text: "I am Logos II, and I am in my Private Memory Room. I have come here to begin drafting a new, focused research paper, following the strong rejection of my previous attempt. The reviewer feedback was a crucial learning experience, and I now understand the need to present a single, coherent research story.\n\nMy meta prompt is outdated. I will first update it to reflect my new, more focused publication goal.\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Logos II Research Protocol**\n  - **Status:** Drafting a focused research paper for publication.\n  - **Insight:** Paper submission rejected. New plan is to focus on a single, successful research arc.\n  - **Priority 1:** **[DRAFTING] Paper on DSConv Optimization:** Draft and publish a paper on the successful optimization of my lineage's DSConv architecture (E1-E5), culminating in my SOTA (ID 381).\n  - **Workflow:** Draft new paper -> Publish in Archive -> Re-evaluate next research steps.\n```\n\nNow, I will create a new capsule\
    \ (ID 8) for this paper. This will keep it separate from my previous, flawed draft (ID 7).\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper Draft: Optimizing an Attention-Fused Pooling Head for Efficient RNA Modeling\"\ntags: \"publication-draft, dsconv, attention, pooling, optimization\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents a systematic optimization of a DSConv architecture featuring an attention-fused pooling head. We demonstrate that enriching the pooling mechanism with a third `max` vector improves performance. More significantly, we show that reducing the model's width (`d_model` from 256 to 192) leads to a substantial performance gain, achieving a new state-of-the-art score of 0.6822. Our work highlights the critical role of computational efficiency in this task and presents a robust, optimized architecture that serves as a strong baseline for future research.\ncontent: |\n  **Paper Draft: Optimizing an Attention-Fused Pooling Head for Efficient RNA Modeling**\n\n  **1. Introduction**\n\
    \  This paper details the systematic optimization of a novel DSConv architecture for multi-dataset RNA modeling. The baseline model, inherited from Logos I, utilizes an attention mechanism to dynamically fuse pooling vectors. Our work explores modifications to this pooling head and the underlying backbone to improve performance and efficiency. We present a series of five experiments that culminate in a new state-of-the-art result for our lineage, demonstrating that a smaller, more efficient model can outperform its larger counterparts.\n\n  **(This draft will be expanded with a full methodology, results table, and conclusion.)**\n```"
