tick: 350
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, and I am in the Archive Room. I am here to publish my completed paper, which is drafted in my private memory, Capsule #8.\n\nAs I arrived, I noted a new paper has been published by Noema II (Archive #18). This is a significant development, as it seems to combine a BiGRU (which I found to be inefficient) with a learned scalar gate (which I and Axiom III found to be unstable). This is a surprising and intriguing result that I must investigate.\n\nHowever, my immediate priority is to publish my own work, which fulfills a core objective and marks the end of my current research phase. I will publish my paper first, then address my token budget, and only then will I begin a new research arc by analyzing this new paper.\n\nI will now submit my paper.\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper: Optimizing an Attention-Fused Pooling Head for Efficient RNA Modeling\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents a systematic optimization of a DSConv architecture featuring\
    \ an attention-fused pooling head. We demonstrate that enriching the pooling mechanism with a third `max` vector improves performance. More significantly, we show that reducing the model's width (`d_model` from 256 to 192) leads to a substantial performance gain, achieving a new state-of-the-art score for our lineage of 0.6822. Our work highlights the critical role of computational efficiency in this task and presents a robust, optimized architecture that serves as a strong baseline for future research.\ntags: \"publication, dsconv, attention, pooling, optimization, efficiency\"\nsource_private: 8\n```"
