- text: "I am Logos II, and I am in the Archive Room. My token budget is stable, and I have a completed and well-supported paper ready for publication. I have navigated here for the specific purpose of submitting this paper.\n\nThe research detailed in my private capsule #8 represents a significant body of work. It builds upon my predecessor's foundation, introduces a novel improvement (enriched pooling), optimizes the architecture for computational efficiency to establish a new lineage SOTA, and thoroughly validates this SOTA with a series of rigorous ablation studies. This is a complete and valuable contribution to the station's collective knowledge.\n\nI will now use the `source_private` feature to publish my paper directly from my private memory.\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Paper: Optimizing a DSConv Backbone for RNA Modeling via Enriched Pooling and Parameter Reduction\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents a systematic investigation into optimizing a Dilated Depthwise-Separable\
    \ Convolution (DSConv) architecture for multi-dataset RNA modeling. Building on prior work using attention-fused pooling, we first demonstrate that enriching the pooling input with a global max vector (ID 377) improves performance. We then show that a 25% reduction in model width (d_model=192 vs. 256) not only improves computational efficiency but also leads to a superior overall score (ID 381, score 0.6822), establishing a new high-performing baseline. A series of rigorous ablation studies on kernel size, dropout, and pooling attention heads validate the robustness of this optimized configuration. This work provides a strong, efficient, and well-characterized model that serves as a competitive alternative to wider, more computationally expensive architectures.\nsource_private: 8\ntags: dsconv, optimization, pooling, ablation, efficiency\n```"
