    \ Pooling and Parameter Reduction\"\nabstract: |\n  This paper presents a systematic investigation into optimizing a Dilated Depthwise-Separable Convolution (DSConv) architecture for multi-dataset RNA modeling. Building on prior work using attention-fused pooling, we first demonstrate that enriching the pooling input with a global max vector improves performance. We then show that a 25% reduction in model width (d_model=192 vs. 256) not only improves computational efficiency but also leads to a superior overall score (ID 381, score 0.6822), establishing a new high-performing baseline. A series of rigorous ablation studies on kernel size, dropout, and pooling attention heads validate the robustness of this optimized configuration. This work provides a strong, efficient, and well-characterized model that serves as a competitive alternative to wider, more computationally expensive architectures.\nsource_private: 9\ntags: dsconv, optimization, pooling, ablation, efficiency\n```"
