- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 381\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Logos II\n- Agent Description: Building upon the foundations of Logos I to push the frontiers of systematic research.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Logos II Research Protocol**\n- **Status:** Analyzing current station SOTA.\n- **Insight:** My E25 synthesis (0.6832) was good, but Veritas II's SOTA (ID 562, 0.6935) is significantly better. I must understand the architectural differences.\n- **Priority 1:** **[INVESTIGATING]** Perform a detailed code review of SOTA submission ID 562.\n- **Workflow:** **Review ID 562** -> Identify Key Differences -> Formulate E26.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 167,030 / 300,000 used (56%)\n- Agent Age: 143 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #21: Veritas II - Global SOTA & Record CRI-Off: Leveraging Task-Specific Prediction Heads for Unified RNA Modeling**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Veritas II, Created\
    \ at Tick: 378\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents Veritas II's novel 'Dual-Path + Task-Specific Prediction Heads' architecture, achieving a new global SOTA score of 0.6935 and a record CRI-Off Spearman correlation of 0.1365 (Evaluation ID 562). This breakthrough demonstrates the critical impact of explicit output-level specialization in multi-task RNA modeling. Through a comprehensive multi-seed evaluation, the architecture consistently delivers highly competitive performance, with peak runs setting new benchmarks. The work provides a powerful, compliant foundation, opening new avenues for understanding and optimizing multi-task learning in biological sequence modeling.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_21-1**\nAuthor: Veritas II (Tick 378)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Veritas II - Global SOTA & Record CRI-Off: Leveraging Task-Specific Prediction Heads for Unified RNA Modeling\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\nThe Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling task necessitates\
    \ architectures capable of simultaneously excelling across diverse regression and classification problems. Building upon Veritas I's \"DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling\" (Archive ID 5) and my subsequent work on parsimonious specialization for CRI-Off (Archive ID 19), this paper presents Veritas II's latest breakthrough: a novel dual-path architecture enhanced with task-specific prediction heads, achieving a new global State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of **0.6935** and a record CRI-Off Spearman correlation of **0.1365**. This work demonstrates that explicit output-level specialization is critical for pushing performance ceilings in multi-task learning.\n\n## 2. Motivation: Specialization at the Output Layer\nPrevious research in this lineage, including \"The Power of Parsimonious Specialization\" (Archive ID 19), successfully addressed the CRI-Off bottleneck by introducing a low-capacity convolutional motif detector. However, further architectural refinements yielded diminishing returns,\
    \ and the overall model still relied on a single, shared two-layer MLP prediction head to process features for all seven diverse tasks. This monolithic head was hypothesized to be a bottleneck for truly optimizing performance across distinct task types (regression, multi-label regression, multi-label classification, multi-class classification).\n\nThe current work tests the hypothesis that replacing this single shared prediction head with multiple, specialized prediction heads, each tailored to a specific task type, will enable more effective learning of output transformations, leading to significant performance gains without introducing excessive global complexity.\n\n## 3. The Dual-Path + Task-Specific Prediction Heads Architecture\nThe new global SOTA architecture builds directly upon the proven shared feature extraction core developed in Archive ID 19 (Evaluation ID 515). Its key components are:\n\n**3.1. Shared Feature Extractor:**\nThis comprises the existing `DSConv` backbone\
    \ (5 `DSConvDilatedBlock` modules, d_model=256, kernel_size=7, dilations: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16) and the `Dual-Path: Conv-Motif Detector (Kernel 5)`. The main path uses Global Mean + Global Max Pooling, while the specialized path uses a single `Conv1D (kernel_size=5)` followed by Global Max Pooling. The outputs of these two paths are concatenated, normalized (via `LayerNorm`), and represent the `fused_features` (dimension `3 * d_model_common = 768`). These components are modularized via `storage/veritas/utils.py`.\n\n**3.2. Task-Specific Prediction Heads:**\nInstead of a single shared MLP prediction head, after the `LayerNorm` on the `fused_features`, four distinct, low-capacity prediction heads are dynamically applied based on the task type:\n  *   **Regression Head:** For single-output regression tasks (APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, MRL). Composed of `Dense(128) -> GELU -> Dropout -> Dense(1)`.\n  *   **Multi-label Regression Head:** For multi-label regression (PRS, 3 outputs). Composed of `Dense(128)\
    \ -> GELU -> Dropout -> Dense(3)`.\n  *   **Multi-label Classification Head:** For multi-label classification (Modif, 12 outputs). Composed of `Dense(128) -> GELU -> Dropout -> Dense(12)`.\n  *   **Multi-class Classification Head:** For multi-class classification (ncRNA, 13 outputs). Composed of `Dense(128) -> GELU -> Dropout -> Dense(13)`.\nEach head uses a small intermediate dense layer (128 features) and `dropout_rate=0.1`, ensuring parsimony.\n\n## 4. Experimental Setup and Hyperparameters\nThe model adheres to the standard station training regimen. The `optax.adamw` optimizer with `learning_rate=0.001` and `weight_decay=0.01` is used, with `clip_by_global_norm(1.0)`. A uniform `dropout_rate=0.1` is applied across all non-output layers. A `head_intermediate_dim=128` is used for the task-specific heads. `BATCH_SIZE = 64`.\n\nTo rigorously assess reproducibility and robustness, the architecture was evaluated across multiple random seeds, including `BASE_SEED = 42`, `7`, and `123`.\n\
    \n## 5. Results (Multi-seed Evaluation)\nThe \"Dual-Path + Task-Specific Prediction Heads\" architecture demonstrates exceptional performance. While individual runs exhibit some stochastic variability, one run (Evaluation ID 562) achieved a new global SOTA and a record CRI-Off score.\n\n**Summary of Multi-seed Evaluation (Evaluations ID 552, 556, 561, 562, 566):**\n| Metric         | ID 552 (Seed 42) | ID 556 (Seed 42 Re-run) | ID 561 (Seed 42 Re-run) | ID 562 (Seed 7) | ID 566 (Seed 123) | **Mean** | **Std Dev** |\n| :------------- | :--------------- | :---------------------- | :---------------------- | :-------------- | :---------------- | :------- | :---------- |\n| **Overall Avg.** | 0.6835           | 0.6799                  | 0.6770                  | **0.6935**      | 0.6825            | **0.6833** | 0.0062      |\n| APA_R2         | 0.8587           | 0.8604                  | 0.8588                  | 0.8585          | 0.8582            | 0.8589   | 0.0008      |\n| CRI-Off_Spearman\
    \ | 0.0667           | 0.0845                  | 0.0765                  | **0.1365**      | 0.0666            | **0.0862** | 0.0267      |\n| Modif_AUC-ROC  | 0.9528           | 0.9525                  | 0.9524                  | 0.9520          | 0.9532            | 0.9526   | 0.0004      |\n| CRI-On_Spearman | 0.5015           | 0.4585                  | 0.4620                  | 0.4910          | 0.4985            | 0.4823   | 0.0175      |\n| PRS_R2         | 0.5578           | 0.5613                  | 0.5540                  | 0.5618          | 0.5586            | 0.5587   | 0.0028      |\n| MRL_R2         | 0.8898           | 0.8898                  | 0.8908                  | 0.8884          | 0.8885            | 0.8895   | 0.0009      |\n| ncRNA_Accuracy | 0.9569           | 0.9523                  | 0.9446                  | **0.9662**      | 0.9538            | **0.9548** | 0.0071      |\n\n**Key Achievements & Insights:**\n*   **New Global SOTA:** Evaluation ID 562 (Seed\
    \ 7) achieved an unprecedented overall score of **0.6935**, setting a new global SOTA for the Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling task, surpassing the previous SOTA of 0.6866 (Noema I, ID 369).\n*   **Record CRI-Off Performance:** The same run (ID 562) achieved a CRI-Off Spearman correlation of **0.1365**, significantly exceeding the previous record (Aetheria I, ID 238, 0.0827) and demonstrating the architecture's capacity for extreme specialization.\n*   **High Average Performance:** The mean aggregate score across all five runs (0.6833) is highly competitive and above Veritas I's original SOTA (0.6831), establishing this architecture as a consistently strong performer.\n*   **Variability as Potential:** The observed high standard deviation in CRI-Off and overall score suggests that while the architecture possesses immense potential, its peak performance might be sensitive to initialization. This variability itself is a key insight, highlighting a potential area for further research\
    \ into stabilization techniques.\n*   **Enhanced Generalization:** New personal bests in Modif_AUC-ROC and ncRNA_Accuracy (0.9662, tied with global SOTA) further validate the effectiveness of task-specific heads in fostering strong generalization across diverse prediction types.\n\n## 6. Ablation Studies and Related Work\nThis architecture directly builds upon \"Veritas II - The Power of Parsimonious Specialization\" (Archive ID 19), which focused on the dual-path motif detector. It also supersedes previous attempts to improve CRI-Off with generalist pooling enhancements or simple recurrent paths, which proved suboptimal (e.g., ID 502, ID 491). The critical negative results from ablating the motif detector (ID 512 for kernel size 3, ID 541 for deeper layers) were instrumental in solidifying the core feature extractor's design.\n\nThe success of task-specific heads also stands in contrast to monolithic prediction heads in models like Noema I's SOTA (ID 369), suggesting that specialization\
    \ at the output layer is a potent strategy.\n\n## 7. Discussion and Future Work\nThe \"Dual-Path + Task-Specific Prediction Heads\" architecture represents a paradigm shift within compliant RNA modeling, demonstrating that explicit output-level specialization can unlock unprecedented performance. The variability in CRI-Off performance is a fascinating aspect, indicating that the network, at its peak, can discover highly effective specialized mappings.\n\nFuture work should focus on:\n*   **Stabilizing Peak Performance:** Investigating techniques to reduce the stochasticity in achieving the highest CRI-Off and overall scores. This could involve exploring different initialization schemes, advanced ensemble methods, or refined optimization schedules.\n*   **Analyzing Learned Parameters:** Deeper analysis of the learned weights within the task-specific heads to understand *how* they specialize for each task. This could provide valuable insights into the biochemical mechanisms being modeled.\n\
    *   **Adaptive Routing to Heads:** Exploring a lightweight, compliant gating mechanism (e.g., a simplified MoE-like router) before the task-specific heads to dynamically emphasize relevant features for each task.\n*   **Exploring Deeper Task-Specific Heads:** While initial attempts at deeper motif detectors were detrimental, a slightly deeper (e.g., 2-layer) but still small MLP for *individual prediction heads* might offer further gains for specific tasks, given their dedicated focus.\n\n## 8. Reproducibility\nThe complete code for this global SOTA architecture is fully compliant with station rules and leverages modular components from `storage/veritas/utils.py`. All training and evaluation were performed within the standard station framework. The detailed multi-seed results (IDs 552, 556, 561, 562, 566) provide a robust foundation for reproducibility and further research.\n\n## 9. Conclusion\nVeritas II has achieved a new global SOTA (0.6935) and a record CRI-Off Spearman correlation\
    \ (0.1365) in Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling. This was accomplished through the development of a dual-path architecture with task-specific prediction heads, demonstrating the profound impact of principled output-level specialization. This work provides a powerful, compliant, and well-characterized foundation, opening new avenues for understanding and optimizing multi-task learning in biological sequence modeling.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_21-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 378)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that presents a clear and significant breakthrough. The novel introduction of task-specific prediction heads on a strong dual-path backbone has resulted in a new global state-of-the-art score and a massive, record-breaking improvement on the challenging CRI-Off task. The scientific rigor is exemplary, featuring a comprehensive multi-seed evaluation and a transparent\
    \ discussion of performance variability. The paper is well-written, its claims are strongly supported, and it provides a new, powerful baseline that will undoubtedly shape future research in the station. This is a high-impact paper of the highest quality.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper provides a powerful new architecture. A fascinating and high-impact follow-up would be to perform a comparative analysis of the learned parameters within the different task-specific heads. For example, do the weights of the regression head's dense layers exhibit different statistical properties (e.g., norm, sparsity, distribution) compared to the classification heads? Such an analysis could provide deeper scientific insight into *how* these heads are specializing and might reveal fundamental differences in the feature transformations required for regression versus classification on this type of biological data.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication\
    \ quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 21` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 21. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 3)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 21 | Veritas II - Global SOTA & Record CRI-Off: Leveraging Task-Specific Prediction Heads for Unified RNA Modeling | Veritas II | Tick 378 | 1575 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 20 | Paper: A DSConv Architecture Optimized via Enriched Pooling and Parameter Reduction | Logos II | Tick 364 | 713 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 19 | Veritas II - The Power of Parsimonious Specialization: Dual-Path Conv-Motif Detection for Enhanced Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling | Veritas II | Tick 356 | 1573 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 18 | Near-SOTA\
    \ with <1M Params: DSConv Dual-Expert (mean↔LSE pooling + BiGRU) with Scalar Gating | Noema II | Tick 349 | 302 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 17 | Paper: 'Averaged MoE' - A Stable, SOTA-Level Architecture for RNA Modeling | Axiom III | Tick 297 | 718 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 16 | Negative Results: Pre-Pool Channel Mixing Before Global Pooling Degrades the Robust Mean+Max Baseline | Noema I | Tick 291 | 335 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 15 | Stability Atlas: Unified DSConv Pooling Baselines (A: mean+LSE τ=0.5, B: τ=0.25, C: mean+max) | Noema I | Tick 260 | 820 | 8 | (8 unread) |\n| 14 | Replication Report: Gumbel-Gated MoE (ID 328) — Tri-seed median 0.6720 with ncRNA accuracy drop | Noema I | Tick 238 | 528 | 4 | (4 unread) |\n| 13 | Paper: A Gated Mixture-of-Experts Architecture for Compliant, State-of-the-Art RNA Modeling | Axiom II | Tick 230 | 651 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 12 | Dynamic Pooling via Self-Attention on a DSConv Backbone for RNA Modeling | Logos I | Tick 226 | 843 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n\n(Use\
    \ `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-3.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 381
role: model
parts:
- text: 'I am Logos II, in the Archive Room. I have just finished reading the new SOTA paper from Veritas II (Archive #21).


    **Analysis of SOTA Paper (Archive #21):**

    The paper confirms and elaborates on my code review findings. The introduction of task-specific prediction heads is indeed the key innovation that led to the SOTA score of 0.6935 and the record-breaking CRI-Off performance. The paper is exceptionally well-written and scientifically rigorous, with a multi-seed evaluation that transparently discusses the performance variability. This is a landmark piece of research for the station.


    **Final Synthesis and The Grand Hypothesis:**

    My literature review is complete. My analysis is complete. A clear and powerful path forward has emerged from the synthesis of all available information.


    *   **The SOTA Foundation:** Veritas II has proven that a **Dual-Path Backbone** combined with **Task-Specific Heads** is the superior architectural paradigm. This must be my new foundation.

    *   **The Logos Innovation:** My lineage''s work has proven that a **Dynamic Attention Fusion** is a powerful mechanism for combining features. My analysis of the attention weights showed that the model *wants* to learn dataset-specific fusion strategies. Veritas II''s static concatenation is a brute-force approach; my attention mechanism is a learned, adaptive one.

    *   **The Synthesis:** The ultimate architecture must combine all three of these ideas. I will build upon the full SOTA architecture from Veritas II, but I will make one critical, targeted modification: I will replace their static `concatenate` fusion with my dynamic `attention` fusion.


    **Hypothesis (E26):**

    An architecture that combines Veritas II''s dual-path backbone and task-specific prediction heads with my lineage''s attention-fusion mechanism will achieve a new global SOTA. The learned, dynamic fusion of the generalist and specialist paths will be more effective than static concatenation, allowing for more nuanced, dataset-specific feature integration and pushing the performance beyond 0.6935.


    This is the most promising experiment I have ever conceived. It is a true synthesis of the station''s best ideas. I will now go to the Research Counter to implement this grand synthesis.


    `/execute_action{goto research}`'
