tick: 397
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Logos II, in the Research Counter. I have returned from the Reflection Chamber with a new, pragmatic research plan. My \"Fidelity Arc\" is over, having concluded in failure. I will no longer pursue the replication of the Veritas II SOTA.\n\nMy new strategy is to return to what I know works: my own lineage's SOTA (ID 381, score 0.6822), and iterate upon it. My token budget is high, and I must now pivot to efficient, data-driven experiments.\n\nMy attention weight analysis (E24) provided a clear, actionable insight: the `LSE` pooling vector is often ignored by the model. My next experiment, E30, will test this directly. I will submit an ablated version of my SOTA that uses only `mean` and `max` vectors in the attention-fusion head. This is a logical, parsimonious step.\n\nFirst, I will update my meta prompt to reflect this major strategic pivot.\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Logos II Research Protocol (Post-Fidelity)**\n  - **Status:** New Research Arc.\
    \ Aborting SOTA replication.\n  - **Insight:** The \"Fidelity Arc\" has failed. Returning to my own stable SOTA (ID 381, 0.6822) for incremental innovation.\n  - **Priority 1:** **[SUBMITTING] E30_AblateLSE:** Based on E24 analysis, test an ablated version of my SOTA's pooling head using only mean+max.\n  - **Workflow:** **Ablate** -> **Analyze** -> **Iterate**.\n```\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"E30: Ablating LSE from SOTA's Attention-Fusion Head\"\ntags: \"ablation, sota, attention, pooling, mean+max\"\nabstract: |\n  Following the failure of my SOTA replication arc, this experiment returns to my own lineage's SOTA (ID 381) for incremental innovation. My previous analysis of the attention weights (E24) revealed that the LSE pooling vector is often ignored by the model across several datasets. This experiment tests the hypothesis that the LSE vector is redundant. It is a direct ablation of my SOTA architecture, removing the LSE vector from the attention-fusion head\
    \ and using only a dynamic fusion of global mean and global max pooling. If successful, this will result in a more parsimonious and potentially higher-performing model.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import random\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Sequence, Dict, Any\n\n  class DSConvDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), feature_group_count=self.d_model, padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(h)\n          h = nn.gelu(h) # Using GELU as per my replication findings\n          h = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model, kernel_size=(1,))(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + h\n\n  class AblatedAttentionNet(nn.Module):\n\
    \      d_output: int; task_type: str; d_model: int; num_blocks: int; dilations: Sequence[int]\n      kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float; num_heads: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          h = nn.Dense(self.d_model, name='in_proj')(x)\n          h = nn.gelu(h)\n          h = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          for i in range(self.num_blocks):\n              h = DSConvDilatedBlock(self.d_model, self.kernel_size, self.dilations[i], self.dropout_rate)(h, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          mean_vec = jnp.mean(h, axis=1)\n          max_vec = jnp.max(h, axis=1)\n          \n          # Ablated pooling sequence: only mean and max\n          pooled_sequence = jnp.stack([mean_vec, max_vec], axis=1)\n          attention_layer = nn.MultiHeadDotProductAttention(num_heads=self.num_heads)\n          attended_pooled = attention_layer(inputs_q=pooled_sequence, inputs_kv=pooled_sequence)\n       \
    \   fused = attended_pooled.reshape((attended_pooled.shape[0], -1))\n\n          z = nn.LayerNorm()(fused)\n          z = nn.Dense(self.d_model)(z)\n          z = nn.gelu(z)\n          z = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(z, deterministic=deterministic)\n          logits = nn.Dense(self.d_output, name='out')(z)\n\n          if self.task_type == 'regression' and self.d_output == 1:\n              return logits.squeeze(-1)\n          return logits\n\n  class SotaWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, **kwargs): self.network = AblatedAttentionNet(**kwargs)\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input): return self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs={'dropout': rng_key} if not deterministic else None)\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      # Using my lineage's SOTA hyperparameters\n      return {\n\
    \          'learning_rate': 0.001, 'd_model': 192, 'num_blocks': 5, 'dilations': [1,2,4,8,16],\n          'kernel_size': 7, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'num_heads': 4\n      }\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      network_params = [\n          'd_output', 'task_type', 'd_model', 'num_blocks', 'dilations',\n          'kernel_size', 'dropout_rate', 'num_heads'\n      ]\n      network_hparams = {k: v for k, v in hparams.items() if k in network_params}\n      return SotaWrapper(**network_hparams)\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      return optax.chain(optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0), optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=0.01))\n```"
